// Kiddo School — Class 24 · Transportation & Vehicles: /learn/ lessons. One
// step-by-step teaching lesson per spotter card. The original flashcard sits
// LEFT with the drawing canvas; this teaching sits RIGHT. Written fresh — the
// library pages in data-transportation.mjs answer WHAT the vehicles are; no
// text is shared. Each card carries FOUR labeled vehicles, so every lesson
// teaches four names and one big spotting idea. The vocabulary follows the
// early-years transport theme words families search in the US, Canada,
// Australia and New Zealand: community vehicles, things that go, land/air/
// water transport.
export const lpContent={
 'land-01':{
  title:'The Car, Bus, Train & Bicycle Lesson',one:'Anchor the four everyday rides — then send the family hunting for all of them.',
  seoTitle:'Car, Bus, Train & Bicycle Lesson for Ages 4–5 — The Everyday Rides (Class 24)',
  metaDescription:'A step-by-step transportation lesson for ages 4–5: teach car, bus, train and bicycle with a ride-report game and a window-spotting hunt that checks off all four.',
  intro:'This lesson opens Class 24 with the four rides almost every family meets in one week — and turns them into a spotting habit that runs all term.',
  what:'Car, bus, train and bicycle are the anchor vehicles of the transport theme: one private, one public, one on rails, one on legs. Every other card in this class is a cousin of one of these four.',
  mean:'Children learn categories by anchoring: the car anchors "roads," the train anchors "rails," the bus anchors "shared rides," the bicycle anchors "people power." Every new vehicle lands on a familiar hook.',
  know:'You need the card, a window with a view or a walk planned, and the four names said slowly. The hunt — spot one of each — can take a week. That is the point.',
  why:'These four are personal: the family car, the school bus, the bike in the yard. Vocabulary anchored in a child\u2019s own life sticks — which is why this card opens the class.',
  dev:'Category anchoring (road/rail/people-power), counting wheels, first-person narrative ("WE ride the bus"), and the observation habit that spotting builds.',
  vocab:'car, bus, train, bicycle, ride, wheels, tracks',
  teach:'1) Name the four, one by one: "car — WE ride in a car. Bus — it carries lots of people. Train — it runs on tracks. Bicycle — YOUR legs make it go."\n2) The wheel count: car four, bus six, train MANY, bicycle two. Count them on the card.\n3) The ride report: "how did YOU get here today? Who came by bus?" Every answer is a word landing.\n4) Set the hunt: "this week, spot all four! A car… a bus… a train… a bicycle. Tell me each time!"\n5) Debrief at the end of the week: which was hardest to find? (Usually the train — a good reason to visit the station.)',
  say:'"Car! Bus! Train! Bicycle! The car is ours, the bus carries everyone, the train runs on its tracks, and the bicycle goes on YOUR power. This week we hunt all four — who spots the train first?"',
  notice:'On this card the heading "Land" sits over four labeled pictures: a blue car, a city bus, a fast train and a bicycle — the everyday fleet, drawn bright.',
  ask:'"Which one have YOU ridden?" "How many wheels on each?" "What makes a train different?" (the tracks!) "Which will we spot first?"',
  childSay:'Your child might report every family ride, count wheels on sighted cars, or demand the station visit — the anchor words, working.',
  real:'All four live on your street: cars everywhere, a bus stop, a bike rack, tracks across town. The card turns errands into field work.',
  play:'Ride Bingo: a hand-drawn card with four squares — car, bus, train, bicycle. Fill the card by the weekend. Winner picks the next walk\u2019s route.',
  check:'Ask: "Name the four rides." "Which runs on tracks?" "Which one needs YOUR legs?" — four names, one rails answer, one legs answer: anchored.'},
 'land-02':{
  title:'The Truck, Motorcycle, Tractor & Ambulance Lesson',one:'Teach vehicles as tools — each one shaped by its job.',
  seoTitle:'Truck, Motorcycle, Tractor & Ambulance Lesson for Ages 4–5 — Wheels That Work (Class 24)',
  metaDescription:'A step-by-step transportation lesson for ages 4–5: teach truck, motorcycle, tractor and ambulance with a match-the-job game that introduces vehicles as tools.',
  intro:'This lesson teaches the big idea that vehicles are tools: the truck hauls, the motorcycle zips, the tractor pulls, the ambulance rushes — each body shaped by its work.',
  what:'Four working vehicles: a cargo truck, a motorcycle, a farm tractor and an ambulance — each designed for exactly one kind of job.',
  mean:'"Vehicles are tools" is the deepest idea in the transport theme: it turns naming into reasoning. Why does the tractor have huge wheels? Ask its job. Why is the ambulance white with a siren? Ask its job.',
  know:'You need the card and the job list: haul, zip, pull, help. Match each vehicle to its verb, then hunt for real examples doing real jobs.',
  why:'Job-matching is the first step from naming to explaining — the thinking skill that makes the whole rest of the class make sense.',
  dev:'Function reasoning (shape follows job), verb vocabulary (haul, pull, rush), community-helpers seeding (the ambulance\u2019s crew), and sorting.',
  vocab:'truck, motorcycle, tractor, ambulance, haul, pull, rush',
  teach:'1) Four names first: "truck… motorcycle… tractor… ambulance."\n2) The verb match: "who HAULS furniture? (truck) Who ZIPS through traffic? (motorcycle) Who PULLS the plow? (tractor) who RUSHES to help? (ambulance)"\n3) The why-shape question: "why does the tractor have such BIG wheels?" — muddy fields! The job wrote the body.\n4) The helper hand-off: "the ambulance is a first-aid kit with wheels — who rides inside?" (paramedics — see Class 33!)\n5) The road hunt: spot a truck hauling, a tractor (or a mower!), an ambulance\u2019s lights. Jobs, everywhere.',
  say:'"Truck! Motorcycle! Tractor! Ambulance! The truck hauls, the motorcycle zips, the tractor pulls, the ambulance rushes to help. Every vehicle has a JOB — and its body is the answer. What job do you see?"',
  notice:'On this card four labeled pictures sit under the heading "Land": a blue truck, a motorcycle, an orange tractor and a white-and-blue ambulance.',
  ask:'"Who hauls? Who pulls?" "Why big wheels on the tractor?" "What does the ambulance do — and who drives it?"',
  childSay:'Your child might assign jobs to every vehicle in sight, explain the tractor\u2019s wheels to grandma, or siren-imitate the ambulance — tool thinking, running.',
  real:'Working vehicles are everywhere: delivery vans on the street, tractors past the town edge, ambulances on call. Jobs fill the road.',
  play:'Job Sort: toy vehicles vs picture cards — match each to a job card (haul/zip/pull/help). Wrong matches are funnier and more instructive than right ones.',
  check:'Ask: "Match the four to their jobs." "Why is the ambulance fast?" "What pulls the plow?" — verbs landing on the right wheels: tool thinking, installed.'},
 'land-03':{
  title:'The Tram, Trolleybus, Rickshaw & Cable Car Lesson',one:'Teach guided vehicles — machines that follow wires, rails and ropes.',
  seoTitle:'Tram, Trolleybus, Rickshaw & Cable Car Lesson for Ages 4–5 — City Movers (Class 24)',
  metaDescription:'A step-by-step transportation lesson for ages 4–5: teach tram, trolleybus, rickshaw and cable car — the guided vehicles that follow rails, wires and ropes through a city.',
  intro:'This lesson teaches the city movers that cannot steer: the tram rides rails in the road, the trolleybus holds a stick to the wire, the rickshaw weaves, the cable car climbs on a rope.',
  what:'Four guided vehicles: tram (rails), trolleybus (overhead wire), rickshaw (three wheels, a roof, a horn) and cable car (hanging from a moving rope).',
  mean:'"Guided vehicles" is a category children can SEE: rails you can touch, wires you can trace, ropes you can follow up a mountain. The city\u2019s tracks hide in plain sight.',
  know:'You need the card and the follow-the-line question for each: what does the tram follow? (rails) The trolleybus? (the wire) The cable car? (the rope) The rickshaw? (only its driver!)',
  why:'Spotting the hidden lines — rails in the tarmac, wires over the street — turns every city walk into an engineering tour. And the rickshaw belongs to millions of families who ride one daily.',
  dev:'Observation of infrastructure, category building (guided vs free), local-transport pride (the rickshaw!), and the guided/free distinction.',
  vocab:'tram, trolleybus, rickshaw, cable car, rails, wire, rope',
  teach:'1) Four names: "tram… trolleybus… rickshaw… cable car."\n2) The follow question, one by one: "what does the tram follow? RAILS in the road! The trolleybus? A wire — see the stick on its roof! The cable car? A ROPE up the mountain."\n3) The odd one out: "the rickshaw follows NOTHING — its driver steers anywhere! Why is it on this card?" (Discuss — it is the free spirit.)\n4) The walk challenge: find a tram rail, a trolleybus wire, or a cable car this week.\n5) The ride poll: which one would you ride first — and why?',
  say:'"Tram! Trolleybus! Rickshaw! Cable car! The tram rides its rails, the trolleybus holds the wire\u2019s stick, the cable car climbs on a rope — and the rickshaw goes ANYWHERE its driver wants. Which city mover would you ride?"',
  notice:'On this card the heading "Land" sits over a blue tram, a trolleybus with its roof pole, a three-wheeled rickshaw and a cable car on its line — city transport, four ways.',
  ask:'"What does each one follow?" "Which one steers freely?" (the rickshaw!) "Which would you ride up a mountain?"',
  childSay:'Your child might trace tram rails on a walk, spot the trolleybus sticks overhead, or narrate a rickshaw ride from memory — the lines, found.',
  real:'Cities worldwide run all four: trams in Melbourne, trolleybuses in Sofia, rickshaws in Lahore and Dhaka, cable cars in the Alps and Rio. A postcard tour is one conversation long.',
  play:'Guided Line Hunt: on your next walk, count the guided lines you can find — rails, wires, painted lanes. Record the finds with a tally; the city is more built than anyone noticed.',
  check:'Ask: "What does the tram follow?" (rails) "What holds the trolleybus to its wire?" (the stick) "Who steers the rickshaw?" (its driver!)'},
 'land-04':{
  title:'The Fire Engine, Police Car, Tow Truck & Garbage Truck Lesson',one:'Teach the helpers — the four trucks every town runs on.',
  seoTitle:'Fire Engine, Police Car & Garbage Truck Lesson for Ages 4–5 — The Helpers (Class 24)',
  metaDescription:'A step-by-step transportation lesson for ages 4–5: teach fire engine, police car, tow truck and garbage truck — the helper vehicles, their jobs and the kindness built into them.',
  intro:'This lesson teaches the helper fleet: the trucks children love loudest, each doing a job that keeps a town safe, moving and clean.',
  what:'Four community vehicles: fire engine (fires and rescues), police car (safety), tow truck (broken cars) and garbage truck (the weekly crunch).',
  mean:'Helper vehicles are the bridge between this class and the community-helpers class: truck + job + the person who drives it. They are also the easiest kindness lesson with wheels on.',
  know:'You need the card and the four jobs: fight fire, keep safe, rescue cars, take trash. The siren question — "why do they make noise?" — is the depth: to tell everyone to make way for help.',
  why:'Children admire these trucks; admiration is a doorway. Walking through it lands the biggest idea in the theme: vehicles exist to help people.',
  dev:'Community-job vocabulary, siren meaning (make way!), sequencing the garbage truck\u2019s weekly visit, and the helper identity that Class 33 grows.',
  vocab:'fire engine, police car, tow truck, garbage truck, help, siren, rescue',
  teach:'1) Four names and four jobs: "fire engine — fights fires. Police car — keeps us safe. Tow truck — rescues broken cars. Garbage truck — takes the trash away. CRUNCH."\n2) The siren question: "why the loud noise? So everyone hears: HELP IS COMING — make way!"\n3) The weekly rhythm: "which helper visits OUR street? When? Let\u2019s watch for it!"\n4) The gratitude beat: "who drives them? Firefighters, police officers, the tow driver, the bin crew — helpers with trucks."\n5) The spotting week: every helper truck seen gets its name called out. The loudest game in the class.',
  say:'"Fire engine! Police car! Tow truck! Garbage truck! The helpers of our town — fighting fires, keeping us safe, rescuing cars, taking the trash. And the siren says: make way — help is coming! Who are the helpers in YOUR street?"',
  notice:'On this card four labeled trucks sit under the heading "Land": a red fire engine, a police car, a tow truck and a green garbage truck — the town\u2019s fleet.',
  ask:'"Match each truck to its job." "Why do helpers have sirens?" "Which helper visits your street — and when?"',
  childSay:'Your child might narrate the garbage truck\u2019s whole visit, siren through the house for stuffed animals, or thank a real firefighter — helpers, honoured.',
  real:'Helper trucks run on a schedule you can watch: bin day, the fire station open day, the tow truck at the corner. Real helpers beat any worksheet.',
  play:'Helper Rescue: teddy stuck on the "roof" (sofa back) — the toy fire engine and its crew respond. Sirens optional; the ladder (your arm) essential. Rotate the emergencies; rotate the heroes.',
  check:'Ask: "Match the four trucks to their jobs." "What does the siren mean?" (make way — help coming) "Who drives them?" (helpers!)'},
 'land-05':{
  title:'The Scooter, Skateboard, Roller Skates & Unicycle Lesson',one:'Teach you-powered wheels — and the one-wheeled wonder that needs balance.',
  seoTitle:'Scooter, Skateboard & Unicycle Lesson for Ages 4–5 — You-Power Wheels (Class 24)',
  metaDescription:'A step-by-step transportation lesson for ages 4–5: teach scooter, skateboard, roller skates and unicycle — the rider-powered family, with wheel counts from eight to one.',
  intro:'This lesson teaches the powered-by-you family: no engine, no fuel, just the rider — and a wheel count that runs from eight all the way down to one.',
  what:'Four rider-powered rides: kick scooter (two wheels), skateboard (four), roller skates (eight) and unicycle (one glorious wheel).',
  mean:'No-engine transport is the bridge to the sports class: these four ARE balance and push skills. The wheel countdown — two, four, eight… ONE — is the maths hook.',
  know:'You need the card, the wheel count and one leg: the unicycle\u2019s secret is single-leg balance, which every child can practise on the floor with shoes on.',
  why:'The unicycle\u2019s "one" is the punchline children retell for weeks — and the balance practice behind it is genuinely useful for every ride to come.',
  dev:'Counting down (8-4-2-1), single-leg balance, push-glide rhythm, and the engine-vs-legs category split.',
  vocab:'scooter, skateboard, roller skates, unicycle, balance, glide',
  teach:'1) Names and wheel counts: "scooter — two. Skateboard — four. Roller skates — EIGHT. Unicycle… ONE!"\n2) The countdown game: hold up fingers — the child names the ride with that many wheels. One finger = chaos and delight.\n3) The balance lab: stand on one leg like a unicycle rider — five seconds each side, arms out, wobbles welcome.\n4) The push demo: how does a scooter go? A KICK. How does a skateboard go? A PUSH. All four run on the rider.\n5) The safety line: "every one of these wears a helmet — even the unicycle. Especially the unicycle."',
  say:'"Scooter — two wheels! Skateboard — four! Roller skates — eight! Unicycle… ONE. No engine — just YOU: kick, push, clip-clop, balance. Show me your one-wheel balance — wobbling is practicing! And helmets on, always."',
  notice:'On this card the heading "Land" sits over a kick scooter, a skateboard, a pair of roller skates and a unicycle — the you-powered family, wheel counts shown.',
  ask:'"Order them by wheels — most to least." "What powers all four?" (the rider!) "Show me unicycle balance!"',
  childSay:'Your child might count wheels on every scooter at the park, balance on one leg through the hallway, or request the unicycle "for my birthday" — you-power, internalized.',
  real:'The park is the exhibit: scooters, boards and skates in action. Watch one minute and count the pushes — the engine is always a leg.',
  play:'Wheel-Count Relay: cards face down, flip, shout the wheel count, do that many hops. Eight hops for roller skates — the workout is built in.',
  check:'Ask: "How many wheels on each?" "What powers them?" (the rider!) "Show me the one-wheel balance!" — counts, power, balance: the family is learned.'},
 'land-06':{
  title:'The Van, Minibus, Limousine & Convertible Lesson',one:'Teach the car family — same bones, four bodies, each built for its job.',
  seoTitle:'Van, Minibus, Limousine & Convertible Lesson for Ages 4–5 — Cars That Grow (Class 24)',
  metaDescription:'A step-by-step transportation lesson for ages 4–5: teach van, minibus, limousine and convertible — four bodies on one car idea, and the shape-follows-job thinking behind each.',
  intro:'This lesson teaches the car family: one basic car, four different bodies — boxy for cargo, long for stars, roofless for sunshine — and the design question behind every shape.',
  what:'Four cars: delivery van (box for cargo), minibus (seats for a team), limousine (stretched long) and convertible (roof down).',
  mean:'Same basic machine, four answers to four jobs — this is design thinking in vehicle form. The limousine\u2019s length and the convertible\u2019s missing roof both have REASONS.',
  know:'You need the card and the question that unlocks it: "what is it carrying?" Boxes, people, stars, sunshine — the answer explains every body.',
  why:'Children who ask "why is it shaped that way?" start reasoning about design everywhere — spoons, chairs, raincoats. The car family is the friendliest first example.',
  dev:'Design reasoning (shape follows purpose), carry-sorting (cargo vs people), length comparison, and the spotting vocabulary of real traffic.',
  vocab:'van, minibus, limousine, convertible, cargo, stretched, roof',
  teach:'1) Four bodies: "van — a box on wheels for PACKAGES. Minibus — seats for a whole TEAM. Limousine — a car stretched LOOOONG. Convertible — the roof comes OFF!"\n2) The carry question: "what is each one carrying?" Sort aloud: boxes, team, stars, sunshine.\n3) The why: "WHY is the limousine long? For stretching out! WHY no roof? For sunshine! Every shape has a reason."\n4) The street test: next drive, spot all four. Vans are easy; the limousine is the jackpot.\n5) The design challenge: "design YOUR car — what does it carry? Draw it!" (The canvas on this lesson is exactly for this.)',
  say:'"Van! Minibus! Limousine! Convertible! Same car — four bodies: boxes, teams, stars and sunshine. WHY is the limousine long? Its job says so! Design your own — what does YOUR car carry?"',
  notice:'On this card the heading "Land" sits over a blue van, a minibus, a long white limousine and a convertible with its top down — one idea, four bodies.',
  ask:'"What does each car carry?" "Why is one stretched?" "Why has one no roof?" "What would YOUR car carry?"',
  childSay:'Your child might classify every parked car, sketch a limousine six pages long, or explain convertibles to a sibling — design eyes, open.',
  real:'The school run is a museum: vans delivering, minibuses shuttling, the occasional convertible with its roof arguing with the weather. All four in one week.',
  play:'Car Factory: one toy car chassis, four "bodies" built from boxes — swap the body, change the job. The factory ships all four by lunch.',
  check:'Ask: "Match each body to its cargo." "Why the stretch?" "What did YOUR designed car carry?" — cargo, reason, design: the family understood.'},
 'land-07':{
  title:'The Bulldozer, Excavator, Crane & Forklift Lesson',one:'Teach the site\u2019s four verbs — push, dig, lift, carry.',
  seoTitle:'Bulldozer, Excavator, Crane & Forklift Lesson for Ages 4–5 — Push, Dig, Lift (Class 24)',
  metaDescription:'A step-by-step transportation lesson for ages 4–5: teach bulldozer, excavator, crane and forklift — the four verbs every building site runs on, acted out at living-room scale.',
  intro:'This lesson teaches the building site\u2019s heart: four machines, four verbs — push, dig, lift, carry — acted out with cushions, spoons and rope before ever watching the real thing.',
  what:'Four site machines: bulldozer (push), excavator (dig), crane (lift high) and forklift (carry pallets, beep in reverse).',
  mean:'Verbs are the deep structure of the construction cards: every machine in the eleven construction spotter sheets does one of these four things, fancier. Learn the verbs once and the whole construction shelf reads itself.',
  know:'You need the card and acting space: a cushion to push, a spoon to dig, a rope-chair "crane" (grown-up power), a box to carry. Then the site visit — from a safe distance.',
  why:'Acting the verbs builds the category; watching the real machines names it. Order matters: act first, watch second, and the site suddenly makes sense.',
  dev:'Verb vocabulary (push, dig, lift, carry), gross motor imitation, machine-to-verb mapping, and safe-distance observation habits.',
  vocab:'bulldozer, excavator, crane, forklift, push, dig, lift, carry',
  teach:'1) Four machines, four verbs: "bulldozer PUSHES… excavator DIGS… crane LIFTS… forklift CARRIES."\n2) Act each: push the cushion, dig with the spoon, lift teddy high (grown-up crane), carry the box with a beep-beep reverse.\n3) The verb hunt on the card: point at each machine, call its verb — fast rounds, big voices.\n4) The site visit: watch a real site from across the road. "Who is pushing? Who is lifting?" The verbs land on steel.\n5) The bridge: "there are ELEVEN more construction cards in this class — all of them push, dig, lift or carry, somehow. Watch for it!"',
  say:'"Bulldozer PUSHES! Excavator DIGS! Crane LIFTS! Forklift CARRIES — beep, beep, backwards! Four machines, four verbs. Act them with me… now watch a real site: WHO is pushing? WHO is digging? Every machine does a verb!"',
  notice:'On this card the heading "Land" sits over a bulldozer, an excavator with its digging arm, a tower crane and a forklift with its pallet — the site\u2019s four verbs, drawn working.',
  ask:'"Match each machine to its verb." "Which one beeps backwards?" "What did the crane lift on the real site?"',
  childSay:'Your child might verb every machine on the road ("that one CARRIES!"), act the full site at bedtime, or explain sites to grandparents — verbs, welded to wheels.',
  real:'Every town has a site somewhere: fencing, mud, machines. One watched minute from safety teaches more verbs than a week of flashcards.',
  play:'Living-Room Site: cushions = soil, spoon = excavator arm, your arm = crane, shoebox = pallet. Run a full "job" — clear, dig, lift, deliver. Break for beep-beep tea.',
  check:'Ask: "The four verbs?" (push, dig, lift, carry) "Match each machine." "What did the real crane lift?" — verbs first, machines second: the site reads itself.'},
 'land-08':{
  title:'The Horse Cart, Sleigh, Wagon & Handcart Lesson',one:'Teach before-engines — animals, slides and muscles moved the world first.',
  seoTitle:'Horse Cart, Sleigh & Wagon Lesson for Ages 4–5 — Old Roads, New Wheels (Class 24)',
  metaDescription:'A step-by-step transportation lesson for ages 4–5: teach horse cart, sleigh, wagon and handcart — the pre-engine carts that show how transport grew from animals, slides and muscles.',
  intro:'This lesson teaches the carts that came before engines: a horse pulling, a sleigh sliding, a wagon rolling, hands pushing — the whole history of transport in four pictures.',
  what:'Four pre-engine movers: horse-drawn carriage, snow sleigh, wooden wagon and handcart — animal power, slide power and muscle power.',
  mean:'Old-tech cards make new-tech cards make sense: engines are NEW. Before them, the world moved on animals, runners and arms — and all three still work.',
  know:'You need the card and the pull-vs-push experiment: a box of toys pulled on a blanket (wagon) vs pushed (handcart) — which is easier? Why?',
  why:'Children assume the world was always motorized. The sleigh\u2019s runners — wheels that fail on snow, slides that win — is the kind of exception that builds real understanding.',
  dev:'Historical sequencing (then/now), the wheels-vs-runners exception, pull-vs-push physics, and the animal-power category (still alive!).',
  vocab:'horse cart, sleigh, wagon, handcart, pull, push, runners',
  teach:'1) Four old movers: "horse cart — the horse PULLS. Sleigh — it SLIDES on snow. Wagon — four wheels, roll. Handcart — YOUR arms push."\n2) The time question: "engines are about a hundred years old. Before that… how did things MOVE?" — these four are the answer.\n3) The experiment: pull the toy-box on a blanket, then push it — "which was easier? Pulling! That is why the horse walks IN FRONT."\n4) The snow exception: "why does the sleigh have NO wheels? Snow swallows wheels — but slides? Swoosh! Runners beat wheels on snow."\n5) The now-hunt: horse carts still work in some towns, wagons haul on farms, handcarts stack every market. Old tech, still employed.',
  say:'"Horse cart — clip clop, the horse pulls! Sleigh — swoosh, runners on snow! Wagon — roll, roll. Handcart — push, push! Before engines, the world moved on animals, slides and muscles. Pull the box… now push it — which is easier? THAT is why the horse goes in front!"',
  notice:'On this card the heading "Land" sits over a horse-drawn carriage, a red sleigh, a wooden wagon and a handcart — the pre-engine fleet, still proud.',
  ask:'"Who pulled the cart before engines?" "Why no wheels on the sleigh?" (snow!) "Which was easier — pull or push?"',
  childSay:'Your child might pull siblings on blankets (wagon service), explain sleighs to a confused snowman, or point out every horse cart in town — history, rolling.',
  real:'Living history is findable: carriage rides in old towns, farm wagons, the market handcart. One sighting beats four explanations.',
  play:'Blanket Wagon: pull a loaded blanket across the room, then push the same load — time both. The verdict explains the horse\u2019s position and the cart\u2019s whole design.',
  check:'Ask: "How did things move before engines?" "Why runners on the sleigh?" "Pull or push — which won?" — then, why, experiment: history earned.'},
 'land-09':{
  title:'The Metro Train, Monorail, Funicular & Steam Locomotive Lesson',one:'Teach the rail family — under, on one beam, up a rope, and across a century.',
  seoTitle:'Metro, Monorail & Steam Train Lesson for Ages 4–5 — The Rail Family (Class 24)',
  metaDescription:'A step-by-step transportation lesson for ages 4–5: teach metro train, monorail, funicular and steam locomotive — four trains that run under, above, up and across time.',
  intro:'This lesson teaches the rail family by direction: one runs UNDER the city, one ON a single beam, one UP a mountain on a rope — and one puffs across a hundred years.',
  what:'Four rail rides: metro train (underground), monorail (single elevated beam), funicular (cable-hauled up steep slopes) and steam locomotive (the smoking ancestor).',
  mean:'Rails are wheels that cannot wander — and each of these four chose a different direction to solve the same problem. Plus the history pair: steam THEN, metro NOW.',
  know:'You need the card and the direction sort: under, above, up, across. The steam question — "what made the puff?" — opens the then-and-now conversation.',
  why:'Trains are the clearest time machine in transport: same rails, different centuries. Children who can order steam-to-metro can order anything.',
  dev:'Direction vocabulary (under, above, up), rail-category thinking, then/now sequencing, and the smoke-to-electric change story.',
  vocab:'metro, monorail, funicular, steam locomotive, underground, beam, steam',
  teach:'1) Four rails: "metro — UNDER the city! Monorail — ON one beam, up high. Funicular — pulled UP the mountain by a rope. Steam locomotive — CHOO CHOO, with real smoke!"\n2) The direction sort: "under… above… up… across. Same family — four directions!"\n3) The then-and-now: "the steam train is the grandmother of all of them — a hundred years older than the metro. What changed? Smoke out, electricity in."\n4) The ride poll: which rail ride would you take first? (The metro\u2019s under-ground arrival is a favourite.)\n5) The track hunt: rails, beams, the funicular\u2019s cable — find one line that cannot wander.',
  say:'"Metro train — under the ground, whoosh! Monorail — one beam, up high! Funicular — the rope pulls it UP the mountain. Steam locomotive — CHOO CHOO, puff puff, the grandmother of them all! Same rails — four directions. Which would you ride first?"',
  notice:'On this card the heading "Land" sits over a metro train, a monorail on its beam, a mountain funicular and a steam locomotive — the rail family, four directions.',
  ask:'"Which one runs UNDER?" "Which climbs a mountain?" (the funicular!) "Which is the oldest?" (steam!) "What changed?" (smoke out, electricity in)',
  childSay:'Your child might describe metro escalators in detail, spot monorail beams on holiday, or choo-choo with authentic smoke noises — rails, mapped.',
  real:'Rail rides are bookable: one metro trip, one heritage steam line, one hill funicular — a year of rail, three tickets. Grandparents optional but excellent.',
  play:'Direction Sort: toy trains vs drawn cards — sort by direction: under (metro mat), over (block beam), up (ramp + string), across (floor). The floor becomes the whole family.',
  check:'Ask: "Order the four by direction." "Which is oldest?" "What changed from steam to metro?" — directions, history, change: the family complete.'},
 'land-10':{
  title:'The Go-Kart, Dune Buggy, Snowmobile & Quad Bike Lesson',one:'Teach terrain-built machines — sand, snow, track and trail each made their own ride.',
  seoTitle:'Go-Kart, Dune Buggy & Snowmobile Lesson for Ages 4–5 — Adventure Rides (Class 24)',
  metaDescription:'A step-by-step transportation lesson for ages 4–5: teach go-kart, dune buggy, snowmobile and quad bike — four adventure machines, each shaped by the ground it rides.',
  intro:'This lesson teaches the adventure four by their playgrounds: the track kart, the sand buggy, the snow sled and the trail quad — shape follows terrain.',
  what:'Four terrain machines: go-kart (smooth track), dune buggy (sand), snowmobile (snow — runners, not wheels!) and quad bike (any trail four chunky wheels can grip).',
  mean:'This is terrain-matching: each machine is the answer to one ground. The snowmobile\u2019s missing wheels (runners beat wheels on snow — see the sleigh!) is the card\u2019s best link.',
  know:'You need the card and the match: track→kart, sand→buggy, snow→snowmobile, trail→quad. The helmet rule rides with all four.',
  why:'Terrain-matching is a thinking pattern: the ground writes the machine. Children who see it here will see it in skis, boats and shoes.',
  dev:'Terrain vocabulary (track, sand, snow, trail), machine-to-ground matching, the wheels-vs-runners echo, and helmet-habit reinforcement.',
  vocab:'go-kart, dune buggy, snowmobile, quad bike, track, sand, snow, trail',
  teach:'1) Four machines, four playgrounds: "go-kart — the smooth TRACK. Dune buggy — the SAND. Snowmobile — the SNOW. Quad bike — any TRAIL!"\n2) The snow echo: "wait — where are the snowmobile\u2019s WHEELS? Gone! Remember the sleigh? Runners beat wheels on snow. Same trick, engine-powered."\n3) The match game: call a terrain, the child calls the machine — fast rounds.\n4) The helmet line: "every rider on this card wears a helmet. Even on sand. Especially on sand."\n5) The spot: quads and buggies turn up at farms and beaches — terrain machines are findable.',
  say:'"Go-kart zips the TRACK! Dune buggy jumps the SAND! Snowmobile — vrrroom — slides the SNOW, no wheels, just runners! Quad bike grips the TRAIL. The ground built each machine — and every rider wears a helmet!"',
  notice:'On this card the heading "Land" sits over a go-kart, a dune buggy, a blue snowmobile and a quad bike — four machines, four terrains.',
  ask:'"Match machine to terrain." "Why no wheels on the snowmobile?" "What do all four riders wear?" (helmets!)',
  childSay:'Your child might match machines to beaches and snowfields on holiday, or remind the family about helmets at the sand dunes — terrain thinking, riding along.',
  real:'Farms run quads, dunes host buggies, ski fields buzz with snowmobiles, and every fun fair has a kart track. Terrains are findable.',
  play:'Terrain Sort: draw four grounds, four machines on cards — match at speed. Add the sleigh card for the snow double-up: runners, twice!',
  check:'Ask: "Match the four to their grounds." "Why runners on snow?" "What does every rider wear?" — terrain, exception, helmet: the adventure four, complete.'},
 'land-11':{
  title:'The Tractor, Combine Harvester, Road Train & Articulated Bus Lesson',one:'Teach the long giants — and the bend-in-the-middle trick that turns corners.',
  seoTitle:'Road Train & Articulated Bus Lesson for Ages 4–5 — Giants That Bend (Class 24)',
  metaDescription:'A step-by-step transportation lesson for ages 4–5: teach tractor, combine harvester, road train and articulated bus — the longest vehicles, and the articulation trick that bends them round corners.',
  intro:'This lesson teaches the longest vehicles in the set: a tractor, a wheat-eating combine, a three-trailer road train and a bus that bends in the middle — plus the fold-test that explains them all.',
  what:'Four long machines: farm tractor, combine harvester, Australian road train (one truck, three trailers) and articulated (bending) bus.',
  mean:'BIG is the theme, BEND is the secret: a vehicle too long to turn must bend. "Articulated" is a wonderful word that means exactly that — and a paper fold proves it in ten seconds.',
  know:'You need the card, paper and tape for the fold-test, and the Australia story: huge empty roads, one truck doing the work of three.',
  why:'The bend explains both the bus and the road train — one idea, two giants. The combine\u2019s triple job (reap, thresh, blow straw) is the farm\u2019s greatest machine in one pass.',
  dev:'Length comparison and ordering, the articulation concept, paper-craft proof, and the geography of the road train (big skies, empty roads).',
  vocab:'tractor, combine harvester, road train, articulated, bend, trailers',
  teach:'1) Four giants: "tractor — pulls everything. Combine — eats whole fields of wheat! Road train — ONE truck, THREE trailers. Articulated bus — bends in the middle!"\n2) The length order: line them up long to longest on the card.\n3) The fold-test: tape two paper cars with a folded hinge — "now turn the corner… IT BENDS! That is what ARTICULATED means."\n4) The Australia story: "the road train crosses huge empty roads — one truck, three trailers, one long haul."\n5) The combine count: "it reaps the wheat, threshes the grain AND blows the straw out — three jobs, one pass!"',
  say:'"Tractor! Combine harvester — chomp, chomp, the wheat is lunch! Road train — one truck, THREE trailers, crossing Australia! Articulated bus — it BENDS in the middle to turn corners! Long, longer, LONGEST — and the bendy ones fold. Watch: paper bus… turn… it bends!"',
  notice:'On this card the heading "Land" sits over a blue tractor, an orange combine harvester, a triple-trailer road train and a bending articulated bus — the long-haul giants.',
  ask:'"Order them by length." "What does articulated mean?" (bends in the middle) "How many trailers on the road train?" (three!) "What three jobs does the combine do?"',
  childSay:'Your child might fold-paper-bend every long vehicle, count road-train trailers on documentaries, or explain combines at harvest time — giants, handled.',
  real:'Harvest season runs combines past every country road; articulated buses bend through every city; road trains haul across Australia\u2019s outback highways.',
  play:'Paper Articulation: build a three-trailer paper road train with taped hinges, then navigate the sofa. Corners demand bends — the concept proves itself.',
  check:'Ask: "Which is longest?" "What does the bend do?" (turns corners) "Name the three-in-one machine." (combine!) — length, bend, jobs: giants understood.'},
 'land-12':{
  title:'The Coach Bus, School Bus, Double-Decker & Electric Bus Lesson',one:'Teach the bus family — four buses, four jobs, one bell to ring.',
  seoTitle:'School Bus & Double-Decker Lesson for Ages 4–5 — The Bus Family (Class 24)',
  metaDescription:'A step-by-step transportation lesson for ages 4–5: teach coach bus, school bus, double-decker and electric bus — four buses, their jobs, six wheels and the quietest one of all.',
  intro:'This lesson teaches the bus family: the long-distance coach, the yellow school run, the two-floor double-decker and the humming electric — four buses, one bell, endless pretend.',
  what:'Four buses: coach (long journeys), school bus (the morning run), double-decker (two floors, six wheels, London\u2019s signature) and electric bus (quiet, no roar).',
  mean:'Buses are shared rides — the theme\u2019s community heart. The family shows one machine wearing four jobs, and the electric bus adds the future: quiet streets are being built right now.',
  know:'You need the card and the job sort: far journeys, school run, two floors, quiet streets. The wheel count (six on a double-decker!) and the bell are the games.',
  why:'The school bus is personal for most children; the double-decker is exotic; the electric bus is the future arriving. One family, three tenses — past habit, present ride, future street.',
  dev:'Shared-transport vocabulary, job matching, six-wheel counting, sound comparison (roar vs hum), and ticket-punch pretend play.',
  vocab:'coach, school bus, double-decker, electric bus, floors, quiet, bell',
  teach:'1) Four buses: "coach — goes FAR. School bus — brings US to school. Double-decker — TWO floors, climb the stairs! Electric bus — shhh… no engine roar."\n2) The job sort: "who rides the coach? Holiday-makers! The school bus? US!"\n3) The wheel count: "a double-decker needs SIX wheels to carry two floors of people — count them!"\n4) The sound test: "old buses roar. Electric buses HUM — like a fridge with seats. Which street would you rather live on?"\n5) The pretend: chairs in a row, tickets punched, the bell — "stop please!" The family, driven.',
  say:'"Coach — far away! School bus — to school, every morning! Double-decker — two floors, six wheels, climb the stairs! Electric bus — shhh, it HUMS. Ding — stop please! Four buses, one family. Which one is YOUR ride?"',
  notice:'On this card the heading "Land" sits over a coach bus, a yellow school bus, a red double-decker and a green electric bus — the bus family, four jobs.',
  ask:'"Match each bus to its job." "How many wheels on the double-decker?" (six!) "Which bus is quietest?" (electric — it hums!)',
  childSay:'Your child might punch tickets for the whole family, count double-decker wheels on screen, or hum their own electric bus — the family, running routes.',
  real:'Buses pass every school gate: the yellow run, the city hum, the coach to the museum. One week of watching meets the whole family.',
  play:'Bus Route: chairs, tickets, a bell (any ding), stops named after family members. The driver rotates; the double-decker has stairs (cushion). Fares: one hug.',
  check:'Ask: "Match the four buses to their jobs." "Count the double-decker\u2019s wheels." "Which is quiet?" — jobs, wheels, hum: the family on route.'},
 'land-13':{
  title:'The Race Car, Rally Car, Go-Kart Trailer & Armored Vehicle Lesson',one:'Teach fast vs strong — two superpowers, four vehicles.',
  seoTitle:'Race Car & Armored Vehicle Lesson for Ages 4–5 — Speed and Shield (Class 24)',
  metaDescription:'A step-by-step transportation lesson for ages 4–5: teach race car, rally car, go-kart trailer and armored vehicle — fast bodies, strong bodies, and the two superpowers of vehicle design.',
  intro:'This lesson teaches the speed-and-shield card: the race car is fast because it is sleek, the armored vehicle is strong because it is thick — two different answers to two different jobs.',
  what:'Four machines: race car (track speed), rally car (gravel jumps), go-kart trailer (the little racer\u2019s ride to the track) and armored vehicle (protection first).',
  mean:'Fast and strong are two different superpowers: sleek wins races, thickness wins survival. Children who can explain WHY each is shaped its way are doing design reasoning at the highest level.',
  know:'You need the card and the shape question: "why is the race car LOW and POINTY? Why is the armored vehicle THICK and HEAVY?" The go-kart trailer is the support-crew story.',
  why:'Contrast cards teach best in pairs: speed explains sleek, danger explains armor. Both explain why YOUR family car looks like neither — it has a different job.',
  dev:'Design-reasoning contrast, speed/safety vocabulary, the support-crew idea (even racers travel with help), and slow-vs-fast play scenarios.',
  vocab:'race car, rally car, go-kart trailer, armored vehicle, fast, strong, protect',
  teach:'1) The speed pair: "race car — LOW and pointy, built for the track! Rally car — jumps gravel roads, bumps and all!"\n2) The shield: "armored vehicle — a car in a suit of armor. Thick, heavy, SLOW. Why? Its job is protection, not speed."\n3) The shape question: "fast needs sleek… safe needs thick. TWO superpowers — which does YOUR family car choose?" (Neither extreme — its job is carrying US!)\n4) The crew story: "even the little go-kart travels with its own trailer — every racer has a support crew."\n5) The slow-safe game: armored-vehicle walk (slow, steady, strong) vs race-car sprint (fast!) — act both superpowers.',
  say:'"Race car — fast and pointy, zoom! Rally car — jumps the bumps! Armored vehicle — thick and strong, slow and safe. Fast is ONE superpower… strong is the OTHER. And even the go-kart brings its own trailer — every racer has a crew!"',
  notice:'On this card the heading "Land" sits over a low race car, a rally car mid-stride, a go-kart on its trailer and a blue armored vehicle — speed and shield, side by side.',
  ask:'"Why is the race car pointy?" "Why is the armored vehicle slow?" (its job is protection!) "What are the two superpowers?" (fast and strong!)',
  childSay:'Your child might sort every car by superpower, build armored shoebox vehicles, or slow-march like an armored convoy — design reasoning, at play.',
  real:'Race weekends, rally clips and armored convoys on the news — fast and strong are on screen weekly. Name the superpower each time one passes.',
  play:'Superpower Sort: toy vehicles into FAST pile and STRONG pile — then design a vehicle with BOTH (the argument is the lesson). Vote on the winner.',
  check:'Ask: "Name the two superpowers." "Match each vehicle to its power." "Why does the armored one move slowly?" — speed, shield, reason: contrast complete.'},
 'water-01':{
  title:'The Boat, Ship, Sailboat & Submarine Lesson',one:'Teach the floating four — surface riders, wind catchers and one that dives.',
  seoTitle:'Boat, Ship, Sailboat & Submarine Lesson for Ages 4–5 — Things That Float (Class 24)',
  metaDescription:'A step-by-step transportation lesson for ages 4–5: teach boat, ship, sailboat and submarine — the floating four, with a bath-time sink-or-float lab and a wind-powered paper sail.',
  intro:'This lesson opens the water cards with the floating four: boat and ship ride the surface, the sailboat borrows the wind, and the submarine dives under and vanishes.',
  what:'Four water vehicles: boat (small, general), ship (big, ocean), sailboat (wind-powered) and submarine (dives and hides).',
  mean:'Water vehicles travel on no roads at all — the whole surface is the highway. The submarine is the set\u2019s only vehicle that HIDES, and the sailboat is the only one powered by weather.',
  know:'You need the card, a basin or bath, floating and sinking toys, a paper sailboat and breath. The lab runs itself.',
  why:'Float-or-sink is most children\u2019s first science experiment — and this card turns bath night into the lab. Wind-power is the second lesson: weather can drive a vehicle.',
  dev:'Surface vs underwater thinking, wind-power introduction, float-sink experimentation, and size scaling (boat → ship).',
  vocab:'boat, ship, sailboat, submarine, float, sink, wind, dive',
  teach:'1) Four floaters: "boat — small and handy. Ship — BIG, crosses oceans. Sailboat — the wind pushes it! Submarine — dives UNDER the water. Glub glub."\n2) The bath lab: float the toys — "which FLOAT like a boat? Which SINK like… a submarine diving?" Sort and re-sort.\n3) The wind test: paper sailboat, one strong blow — "the WIND moved it! That is how a sailboat works — weather power!"\n4) The dive: duck the submarine toy under — periscope up! "The submarine is the only one that HIDES."\n5) The size ladder: "a boat fits on a trailer. A ship needs a harbour. Same idea — different sizes."',
  say:'"Boat! Ship! Sailboat! Submarine! The boat floats, the ship crosses oceans, the wind pushes the sailboat — whoosh — and the submarine dives down, down, UNDER. Float the toys… blow the sail… dive the submarine. Water transport!"',
  notice:'On this card the heading "Water" sits over a small boat, a big ship, a sailboat with white sails and a round blue submarine — the floating four, drawn afloat.',
  ask:'"Which one dives?" (the submarine!) "What pushes the sailboat?" (the wind!) "Which is biggest?" (the ship) "Which toys floated?"',
  childSay:'Your child might run the float lab nightly, blow boats across every puddle, or dive toys with periscope sounds — water vocabulary, afloat.',
  real:'Harbours, lakes and the bath: boats dock everywhere. Ferry rides, sail sightings and submarine museums are all bookable water lessons.',
  play:'Harbour Sort: bath toys by water type — surface fleet (boats, ships), wind fleet (sails), dive fleet (submarine). Three fleets, one harbour, one captain.',
  check:'Ask: "Name the floating four." "What powers the sailboat?" (wind) "Which one hides?" (submarine!) — surface, wind, dive: water opened.'},
 'water-02':{
  title:'The Ferry, Kayak, Canoe & Speedboat Lesson',one:'Teach the river runners — and the one-paddle spin that explains steering.',
  seoTitle:'Ferry, Kayak & Canoe Lesson for Ages 4–5 — River Runners (Class 24)',
  metaDescription:'A step-by-step transportation lesson for ages 4–5: teach ferry, kayak, canoe and speedboat — river transport, the one-blade/two-blade paddle puzzle and the spin that explains steering.',
  intro:'This lesson teaches the river runners: the patient ferry, the double-blade kayak, the single-paddle canoe and the roaring speedboat — with the paddle puzzle at the centre.',
  what:'Four river vehicles: ferry (back and forth, all day), kayak (double-blade paddle), canoe (single paddle, swapped sides) and speedboat (engine power).',
  mean:'The kayak-canoe pair is a fine paddling puzzle: two blades or one, and WHY one-sided paddling spins you in circles. The spin IS the steering lesson — physics you can act in a chair.',
  know:'You need the card and chair-boats: a stick for a double "kayak" paddle, then a single "canoe" paddle. The spin experiment is the lesson\u2019s heart.',
  why:'One-side-only paddling spinning the boat is real physics, actable in ten seconds — and it explains why canoeists swap sides. Experiments that explain steering beat rules about steering.',
  dev:'Paddle mechanics, the spin-and-correct experiment, patient-route vocabulary (the ferry), and engine-vs-paddle power categories.',
  vocab:'ferry, kayak, canoe, speedboat, paddle, blade, spin, steer',
  teach:'1) Four river runners: "ferry — back and forth, all day, horn and all. Kayak — TWO blades! Canoe — ONE paddle, swap sides! Speedboat — ZOOM, engine power."\n2) The kayak paddle: chair-boat, double stick — "splash left… splash right… straight ahead!"\n3) The spin experiment: single paddle, ONE side only — "we\u2019re going in a CIRCLE! That is why canoeists swap sides — left, right, left!"\n4) The ferry story: "the ferry is the water\u2019s bus — same crossing, every day, carrying everyone."\n5) The power sort: paddles (kayak, canoe) vs engines (speedboat) vs schedule (ferry). Three powers, four boats.',
  say:'"Ferry — back and forth, all day! Kayak — two blades, splash splash! Canoe — one paddle… whoa, SPINNING! Swap sides — left, right — straight again! Speedboat — ZOOM! Rivers were the first roads, and these four still run them."',
  notice:'On this card the heading "Water" sits over a ferry, a yellow kayak with its double paddle, a canoe and a speedboat — river runners, four powers.',
  ask:'"Which paddle has two blades?" "Why does one-side paddling spin you?" "What is the water\u2019s bus?" (the ferry!)',
  childSay:'Your child might chair-paddle through the house, demand side-swaps mid-"canoe," or narrate every ferry crossing — the spin, understood.',
  real:'River towns run ferries; lakes rent kayaks and canoes; speedboats sketch across every bay. One river crossing is a term of lessons.',
  play:'Spin Test: office chair or cushion-scoot, one-arm paddles only — spin! Then two-arm: straight. The physics of steering, demonstrated by the captain.',
  check:'Ask: "Two blades or one — who has which?" "Why swap sides?" (else you spin!) "Who crosses all day?" (the ferry) — paddles, spin, patience: rivers run.'},
 'water-03':{
  title:'The Yacht, Catamaran, Cruise Ship & Lifeboat Lesson',one:'Teach the ocean fleet — two hulls, floating towns and the bravest boat.',
  seoTitle:'Yacht, Catamaran & Lifeboat Lesson for Ages 4–5 — The Ocean Fleet (Class 24)',
  metaDescription:'A step-by-step transportation lesson for ages 4–5: teach yacht, catamaran, cruise ship and lifeboat — the ocean fleet, the two-hull experiment and the hero boat that rescues.',
  intro:'This lesson teaches the ocean fleet: the sailing yacht, the two-hulled catamaran, the cruise ship that is a floating town — and the lifeboat, whose brave job rescues people.',
  what:'Four ocean vehicles: yacht (sailing for joy), catamaran (two hulls — stability!), cruise ship (a thousand rooms) and lifeboat (the rescue specialist).',
  mean:'The catamaran\u2019s two-hull trick is real engineering a child can test with cups; the lifeboat connects water to the helpers theme; the cruise ship answers "how many people fit on a boat?" like a magic trick.',
  know:'You need the card, two cups taped together (the catamaran test), and the lifeboat story told with respect: crews launch in storms to save others.',
  why:'Testing stability (one hull vs two) is engineering with household equipment — and the lifeboat gives the ocean fleet its heart: boats have jobs, and the bravest one wears orange.',
  dev:'Stability experimentation, scale wonder (the cruise ship), helper-boat vocabulary, and hull counting that doubles as engineering talk.',
  vocab:'yacht, catamaran, hull, cruise ship, lifeboat, rescue, brave',
  teach:'1) The fleet: "yacht — sailing for the joy of it! Catamaran — TWO hulls! Cruise ship — a floating TOWN. Lifeboat — the rescuer."\n2) The hull test: float one cup (wobbly!) vs two taped cups (steady!) — "TWO hulls = steadier. That is the catamaran\u2019s secret!"\n3) The scale question: "how many people fit on a cruise ship? THOUSANDS — pools, theatres, a whole town that floats."\n4) The hero: "the lifeboat launches in STORMS to save ships in trouble. The bravest job on the water."\n5) The fleet review: sail power, stability, scale, rescue — four boats, four ideas.',
  say:'"Yacht — sailing on the wind! Catamaran — TWO hulls, steady as a table — test it with cups! Cruise ship — a whole town afloat, a thousand rooms! And the lifeboat — brave crews, storm weather, rescuing people. What a fleet!"',
  notice:'On this card the heading "Water" sits over a white yacht, a two-hulled catamaran, a grand cruise ship and an orange lifeboat — the ocean fleet at anchor.',
  ask:'"How many hulls on a catamaran?" (two!) "Was the two-cup boat steadier?" "What does the lifeboat do?" (rescues!) "How many people fit on a cruise ship?"',
  childSay:'Your child might tape-cup-test every floating toy, count cruise-ship decks on screen, or salute passing lifeboat stations — the fleet, admired.',
  real:'Marinas hold yachts and catamarans, ports berth cruise giants, and every coast keeps its lifeboat station. The fleet is visitable.',
  play:'Cup Fleet: build one-hull and two-hull boats, load "cargo" (coins) — which carries more before tipping? Stability and capacity, tested by the harbourmaster.',
  check:'Ask: "Why two hulls?" (steadier!) "What floats like a town?" (the cruise ship) "Who launches in storms?" (the lifeboat crews!) — stability, scale, bravery: the fleet sails.'},
 'water-04':{
  title:'The Tugboat, Dredger, Hydrofoil & Paddle Steamer Lesson',one:'Teach the harbor specialists — tiny pushers, mud eaters, water-flyers and wheel-walkers.',
  seoTitle:'Tugboat & Hydrofoil Lesson for Ages 4–5 — Harbor Workers (Class 24)',
  metaDescription:'A step-by-step transportation lesson for ages 4–5: teach tugboat, dredger, hydrofoil and paddle steamer — the harbor specialists, the small-pushes-big story and the boat that flies.',
  intro:'This lesson teaches the harbor\u2019s specialists: the tiny tug that moves giants, the dredger that eats mud, the hydrofoil that flies on stilts and the paddle steamer walking on water wheels.',
  what:'Four specialist boats: tugboat (pushes ships many times its size), dredger (digs the bottom), hydrofoil (lifts onto wings and flies) and paddle steamer (the wheeling ancestor).',
  mean:'Specialists break the rules children just learned: small can move big (tug), boats can fly (hydrofoil), wheels work in water (paddle steamer). Exceptions are where understanding deepens.',
  know:'You need the card, a small toy and a big book for the tug test, and the David-and-Goliath framing: tiny boat, giant ship, slow and mighty.',
  why:'The tug test — can small push big? — is physics, character education and the best story in the water set at once. The hydrofoil bridges straight to the Air cards.',
  dev:'Force intuition (small, steady, mighty), exception thinking, the boat-that-flies bridge, and historical wheel power (the steamer).',
  vocab:'tugboat, dredger, hydrofoil, paddle steamer, push, wings, wheels',
  teach:'1) The specialists: "tugboat — tiny but MIGHTY. Dredger — eats the mud so ships can park. Hydrofoil — grows wings and FLIES over water! Paddle steamer — walks on its own water wheels."\n2) The tug test: small toy against a big book across the table — "slowly… steadily… IT MOVES! That is a tugboat\u2019s whole job."\n3) The flyer question: "why would a boat want to LIFT out of the water?" — less splash, more speed. The hydrofoil is a boat doing an airplane\u2019s trick.\n4) The wheels: "the paddle steamer walks on wheels — the steam train\u2019s river cousin, from the same century."\n5) The mud story: "harbours fill with mud. The dredger vacuums it out — or the ships run aground. The least glamorous boat, the most necessary."',
  say:'"Tugboat — toot! Small but mighty: it PUSHES the giant ship! Dredger — schlurp, eating the harbour mud. Hydrofoil — up on its wings, FLYING over the water! Paddle steamer — round and round on its wheels, the oldest dance on the river. Harbor specialists!"',
  notice:'On this card the heading "Water" sits over a blue tugboat, a working dredger, a hydrofoil up on its foils and a paddle steamer mid-wheel — specialists, hard at it.',
  ask:'"Who pushes the giant ships?" (the tugboat!) "What eats the mud?" (the dredger) "Which boat flies?" (the hydrofoil!) "Which walks on wheels?" (the paddle steamer)',
  childSay:'Your child might tug-test every household object, report dredgers on documentaries, or wheel-walk their arms like a steamer — exceptions, loved.',
  real:'Harbours employ all four: tugs escort ships in, dredgers keep channels deep, hydrofoils ferry passengers, and paddle steamers chuff on heritage rivers.',
  play:'Tug Convoy: the small toy "tugs" the cushion-ship across the rug — slow, steady, docking achieved. Add the hydrofoil hand-flight for the finale.',
  check:'Ask: "Small pushes big — who?" (the tugboat) "Which boat flies?" (hydrofoil) "Whose wheels walk the water?" (paddle steamer) — push, fly, wheel: specialists complete.'},
 'air-01':{
  title:'The Airplane, Helicopter, Hot-Air Balloon & Rocket Lesson',one:'Open the sky — wings, blades, breeze and the countdown that ends the lesson.',
  seoTitle:'Airplane, Helicopter & Rocket Lesson for Ages 4–5 — Up Into the Sky (Class 24)',
  metaDescription:'A step-by-step transportation lesson for ages 4–5: teach airplane, helicopter, hot-air balloon and rocket — the famous four of flying, with a paper-plane glide and a five-count blast-off.',
  intro:'This lesson opens the sky with its famous four: wings, spinning blades, drifting breeze and the rocket that leaves the whole sky behind — ending, inevitably, in a countdown.',
  what:'Four flyers: airplane (winged), helicopter (rotor-bladed), hot-air balloon (lighter-than-air drift) and rocket (the only vehicle that leaves the planet).',
  mean:'The sky\u2019s front door: these four are nameable by age three, which makes them the anchor set for every air card after. The rocket\u2019s countdown is also the best transition device in early years.',
  know:'You need the card, a paper airplane, a spinning-hand "helicopter," a drawn balloon and the countdown: five-four-three-two-one, blast off, arms up.',
  why:'Flying is the most wondered-about kind of going. Anchoring wonder on four friendly names turns every contrail into a vocabulary moment.',
  dev:'Air-vehicle vocabulary, glide observation, rotor imitation, countdown sequencing, and the sky-as-a-place category.',
  vocab:'airplane, helicopter, hot-air balloon, rocket, fly, wings, blast off',
  teach:'1) Four flyers: "airplane — big wings! Helicopter — whir-whir-whir on top! Hot-air balloon — floats like a giant bubble. Rocket — 5-4-3-2-1, BLAST OFF!"\n2) The glide test: throw the paper airplane — "wings at work! How far did it fly?"\n3) The rotor: spin one hand over the head — "helicopter blades!" Walk-and-whirr across the room.\n4) The drift: arms wide, sway slowly — "the balloon goes where the wind says. No hurry."\n5) THE COUNTDOWN: five-four-three-two-one… BLAST OFF — everyone crouches, counts, explodes upward. Repeat on request forever.',
  say:'"Airplane! Helicopter! Hot-air balloon! Rocket! Wings, whirring blades, drifting bubbles and… 5-4-3-2-1… BLAST OFF! The rocket is the only one that leaves the whole sky behind. Fly the fleet with me!"',
  notice:'On this card the heading "Air" sits over four labeled flyers: a blue airplane, a helicopter, a striped hot-air balloon and a rocket — the sky\u2019s famous four.',
  ask:'"Which one spins on top?" (helicopter) "Which one floats?" (balloon) "Which leaves the planet?" (rocket!) "Show me the countdown!"',
  childSay:'Your child might blast off at every opportunity, whirr through hallways, or spot contrails as "airplane lines" — the sky, colonised.',
  real:'The sky serves daily: contrails overhead, helicopters over the city, festival balloons, and rocket launches on every screen. Flyovers are free field trips.',
  play:'Airshow: paper planes (glide), hand rotors (hover), balloon arms (drift) and the group countdown (blast off). Four events, one airshow, infinite encores.',
  check:'Ask: "Name the sky\u2019s four." "What spins on a helicopter?" "Show me the countdown!" — wings, blades, breeze, blast-off: the sky is open.'},
 'air-02':{
  title:'The Jet, Seaplane, Glider & Blimp Lesson',one:'Teach four more ways to fly — power, water-landing, engineless and floating.',
  seoTitle:'Jet, Seaplane & Glider Lesson for Ages 4–5 — Four Ways to Fly (Class 24)',
  metaDescription:'A step-by-step transportation lesson for ages 4–5: teach jet, seaplane, glider and blimp — fast wings, lake landings, silent glides and the world\u2019s slowest flying giant.',
  intro:'This lesson stretches "airplane" into a family: the jet that outruns sound, the seaplane that parks on lakes, the glider that flies on silence and the blimp that is in no hurry at all.',
  what:'Four air vehicles: jet (fast, high), seaplane (water landings), glider (no engine — wind only) and blimp (floating giant, slower than a bicycle).',
  mean:'One word — flying — covers four different answers: power (jet), place (seaplane), silence (glider) and slowness (blimp). The glider\u2019s trick — wings work without engines — is real physics a paper plane can prove.',
  know:'You need the card and a paper "glider" thrown gently: how far can silence travel? The blimp riddle: why so slow? (It floats — it is in no hurry.)',
  why:'The glider is the card\u2019s physics star and the blimp is its joke — proof that flying does not always mean fast. Contrast teaches the category better than any single example.',
  dev:'Air-category splitting (power/water/silence/float), glide experiments, sound vocabulary (WHOOSH vs shhh), and gentle-throw motor control.',
  vocab:'jet, seaplane, glider, blimp, glide, float, silent',
  teach:'1) The family: "jet — faster than sound, WHOOSH! Seaplane — lands on LAKES. Glider — no engine, just wind — shhh. Blimp — slow and round, the giant fish of the sky."\n2) The glide test: gentle paper-glider throws — "engine off… how far did SILENCE fly?"\n3) The parking question: "where does a seaplane park? On the lake — no runway needed!"\n4) The blimp riddle: "why is the blimp slowest of all? It FLOATS — flying without hurrying is still flying."\n5) The sound line-up: jet WHOOSH, seaplane splash, glider shhh, blimp… (drumroll of nothing). Four flyers, four sounds.',
  say:'"Jet — WHOOSH, faster than sound! Seaplane — splash, lands on the lake! Glider — shhh, riding the wind with no engine at all. Blimp — the world\u2019s slowest flight, and proud of it. Four ways to fly!"',
  notice:'On this card the heading "Air" sits over a blue jet, a seaplane on its pontoons, a white glider and a round blimp — the air family, four answers.',
  ask:'"Which needs no runway?" (seaplane!) "Which has no engine?" (glider) "Which is slowest?" (blimp!) "How far did your glider fly?"',
  childSay:'Your child might throw whisper-glides on repeat, park toy planes on bath water, or defend the blimp\u2019s honour — the family, split and sorted.',
  real:'Airshows display all four: jets scream, seaplanes scoop, gliders circle silently, blimps drift over stadiums. One airshow covers the family.',
  play:'Glide Contest: same paper, different throws — hard (jet!) vs gentle (glider). Chart which went further. The physics argues for gentle, every time.',
  check:'Ask: "Who lands on water?" "Who flies engineless?" "Who is slowest?" — power, water, silence, float: four ways to fly, sorted.'},
 'air-03':{
  title:'The Drone, Fighter Jet, Cargo Plane & Passenger Plane Lesson',one:'Teach today\u2019s working sky — who carries people, who carries boxes, who flies itself.',
  seoTitle:'Drone & Cargo Plane Lesson for Ages 4–5 — Busy Skies (Class 24)',
  metaDescription:'A step-by-step transportation lesson for ages 4–5: teach drone, fighter jet, cargo plane and passenger plane — the working aircraft, the newest vehicle in the set and the sky as a workplace.',
  intro:'This lesson teaches the sky as a workplace: the hovering drone, the fast fighter, the parcel-swallowing cargo plane and the passenger plane that carries hundreds over oceans.',
  what:'Four working aircraft: drone (remote-controlled, camera-eyed), fighter jet (fast patrol), cargo plane (freight) and passenger plane (hundreds of travelers).',
  mean:'The drone is the NEWEST vehicle in the whole set — most grandparents never saw one as children — which makes this card a then-and-now conversation as much as a vocabulary lesson.',
  know:'You need the card and the job sort: people, parcels, patrol, pictures. The drone-pilot pretend (one hand flies, one hand works the remote) is the game.',
  why:'"The sky is a workplace" reframes every plane in the sky as somebody\u2019s job — and jobs are how children organize the world.',
  dev:'Job sorting (people/parcels/patrol/pictures), the newest-vs-oldest timeline, remote-control concept, and the cargo plane\u2019s nose that opens.',
  vocab:'drone, fighter jet, cargo plane, passenger plane, remote, cargo, passengers',
  teach:'1) The staff photo: "drone — flies ITSELF, from a remote! Fighter jet — fast patrol. Cargo plane — swallows the PARCELS. Passenger plane — carries HUNDREDS of travelers."\n2) The job sort: "who carries people? Parcels? Who takes the pictures? Who patrols fast?"\n3) The timeline: "the drone is the newest vehicle in this whole class — your grandparents\u2019 childhoods had NONE. What else is new?"\n4) The cargo trick: "the cargo plane\u2019s NOSE opens to swallow the freight — like a pelican!"\n5) The pilot pretend: one hand hovers (the drone), one thumb works buttons (the remote) — land it on the sofa, gently.',
  say:'"Drone — hovering, filming, flown from a remote! Fighter jet — WHOOSH, on patrol! Cargo plane — gulp, the parcels go in the nose! Passenger plane — hundreds of travelers, up above the clouds. Today\u2019s sky is a workplace!"',
  notice:'On this card the heading "Air" sits over a small drone, a grey fighter jet, a giant cargo plane and a passenger jet — the working sky, drawn on duty.',
  ask:'"Who carries people? Parcels?" "Which is the newest?" (the drone!) "What opens on the cargo plane?" (the nose!)',
  childSay:'Your child might remote-hand drones through doorways, classify every plane at the airport window, or explain drones to amazed elders — the workplace, staffed.',
  real:'Airports, drone shots on every nature documentary, cargo giants at the freight terminal — the working sky is visible from any window with patience.',
  play:'Air Traffic Control: paper planes as the fleet — cargo (carries a sticker load), passenger (carries a peg person), drone (hand-launched, hovers). The tower (you) clears each for landing.',
  check:'Ask: "Sort the four by cargo." "Which vehicle is newest?" "What does a passenger plane carry?" (people — hundreds!) — jobs, timeline, cargo: the sky, employed.'},
 'air-04':{
  title:'The Space Shuttle, Hang Glider, Gyrocopter & Flying Boat Lesson',one:'Teach the specialists — orbit, breeze, rotor and the boat with wings.',
  seoTitle:'Space Shuttle & Hang Glider Lesson for Ages 4–5 — Sky Specialists (Class 24)',
  metaDescription:'A step-by-step transportation lesson for ages 4–5: teach space shuttle, hang glider, gyrocopter and flying boat — the specialists at the edges of flight, from orbit to duck-landings.',
  intro:'This lesson teaches flight\u2019s edges: the shuttle that rode a rocket to orbit and glided home, the hang glider riding breeze like a bird, the gyrocopter\u2019s free-spinning blade and the flying boat that lands like a duck.',
  what:'Four specialists: space shuttle (orbit and back), hang glider (engineless soaring), gyrocopter (unpowered top rotor) and flying boat (a boat with wings).',
  mean:'Edge-of-flight cards show what flying CAN be: space, silence, simplicity, splashdowns. The shuttle is history now — retired — which makes it a museum conversation as much as a vehicle.',
  know:'You need the card and hang-glider arms: lean, arms wide, glide across the room — the slowest race ever. The space question: which one has been to SPACE?',
  why:'Specialists each solve one problem — space, silence, smallness, water — and solving-problems-by-design is the deepest idea the air cards can leave.',
  dev:'Edge-category thinking, glide imitation (slow bodies!), the retired-vehicle history note, and the duck-landing bridge back to water-01.',
  vocab:'space shuttle, hang glider, gyrocopter, flying boat, orbit, soar, glide',
  teach:'1) The specialists: "space shuttle — rode a rocket to SPACE, glided home like a plane! Hang glider — hangs under a wing, rides the breeze. Gyrocopter — a blade spins above, free! Flying boat — a boat with wings, lands on water like a duck."\n2) The glide: arms wide, lean, cross the room — "slowest race in the class! That is hang-gliding."\n3) The space question: "which one has been to SPACE? The shuttle — and it retired years ago. Museums keep them now."\n4) The bridge: "the flying boat is two vehicles at once — water-01\u2019s cousin with wings!"\n5) The specialist review: space, breeze, rotor, splash — four edges of the sky.',
  say:'"Space shuttle — to orbit and BACK! Hang glider — arms wide, riding the breeze, slowest race ever. Gyrocopter — its top blade spins free in the wind! Flying boat — lands on the lake like a duck. The specialists of the sky!"',
  notice:'On this card the heading "Air" sits over a space shuttle, a hang glider under its wing, a small gyrocopter and a flying boat — the edges of flight, drawn soaring.',
  ask:'"Which one went to SPACE?" (the shuttle!) "Show me the hang-glider glide!" "Which lands like a duck?" (the flying boat!)',
  childSay:'Your child might glide the length of the house, demand the space museum, or duck-land every toy plane in the bath — the edges, explored.',
  real:'Space museums display real shuttles; hills host hang gliders on windy Sundays; airshows fly gyrocopters; lakeside towns watch flying boats at vintage meets.',
  play:'Edge Certificates: four stations — glide (arms), rotor (hand-spin), splash-land (bath), blast-off (countdown) — earn a sticker per station. Certified specialist of the sky.',
  check:'Ask: "Name the four specialists." "Which one is retired?" (the shuttle — museums!) "Show me the glide!" — space, breeze, rotor, splash: edges complete.'},
 'construction-01':{
  title:'The Road Roller, Mixer, Dump Truck & Paver Lesson',one:'Teach the road crew — flatten, mix, dump, lay: how a road is born.',
  seoTitle:'Road Roller & Paver Lesson for Ages 4–5 — The Road Crew (Class 24)',
  metaDescription:'A step-by-step transportation lesson for ages 4–5: teach road roller, concrete mixer, dump truck and asphalt paver — the four machines that build a road, replayed with toys and a paint roller.',
  intro:'This lesson teaches the road crew in order: flatten the ground, mix the concrete, dump the gravel, lay the smooth black road — a full road, built on the living-room floor.',
  what:'Four road-building machines: road roller (flatten), concrete mixer (tumbling drum), dump truck (the load) and asphalt paver (the smooth layer behind it).',
  mean:'This card answers the question every child asks while watching a street get fixed: who does WHAT? Four machines, four steps, one road — a sequence the toys can replay end to end.',
  know:'You need the card, a paint roller (the road roller), a spinning jar (mixer), a toy tipper and a rectangular block (paver). Build the road across the rug.',
  why:'Sequence-building (step one, two, three, four) is the deep skill here — construction is the friendliest place to learn that big jobs are ordered small jobs.',
  dev:'Sequencing (build order), machine-to-step mapping, rolling and tipping motor play, and the road-under-our-feet appreciation.',
  vocab:'road roller, mixer, dump truck, paver, flatten, lay, road',
  teach:'1) The crew in order: "road roller — sllloooow, FLAT. Mixer — round and round. Dump truck — tips the load, CRASH. Paver — lays the road smooth as icing!"\n2) Build it: flatten (roller), mix (jar spin), dump (tipper), lay (block slides forward leaving "road").\n3) The question: "next time a street is dug up — WHO does each step? Watch!"\n4) The roller\u2019s charm: "the roller is a giant rolling pin — for roads instead of cookies."\n5) The road test: drive a toy car along the freshly laid rug-road. The crew inspects its work.',
  say:'"Road roller — FLAT! Mixer — round and round! Dump truck — CRASH, there is the gravel! Paver — smooth, smooth, the road appears! Four machines, four steps, one brand-new road. Now drive on it — the crew approves!"',
  notice:'On this card the heading "Construction Vehicles" sits over a road roller, a concrete mixer truck, a dump truck and an asphalt paver — the road crew, in build order.',
  ask:'"What does the roller do?" (flattens) "Which machine mixes?" "Which lays the road?" "What are the four steps?"',
  childSay:'Your child might build roads across every rug, roll toys flat with rolling-pin reverence, or narrate street works like a foreman — the crew, employed.',
  real:'Road works hire the whole crew in public: rollers idling, mixers turning, pavers crawling. One ten-minute watch from safety covers the card.',
  play:'Roadworks Replay: the full sequence with toys — flatten, mix, dump, lay, DRIVE. Add a "road closed" sign (paper) for authenticity. Reopen when the crew waves through.',
  check:'Ask: "Name the crew in order." "What flattens?" (roller) "What lays the smooth road?" (paver) — flatten, mix, dump, lay: a road is born.'},
 'construction-02':{
  title:'The Wheel Loader, Backhoe, Grader & Skid-Steer Lesson',one:'Teach the scoop squad — four machines, one great bite.',
  seoTitle:'Wheel Loader & Skid-Steer Lesson for Ages 4–5 — The Scoop Squad (Class 24)',
  metaDescription:'A step-by-step transportation lesson for ages 4–5: teach wheel loader, backhoe loader, motor grader and skid-steer loader — the scoop squad, with a pom-pom gravel relay and a pirouette finish.',
  intro:'This lesson teaches the scoop squad: four machines built around moving material — big bites, back arms, flat shaves and one pirouetting dancer.',
  what:'Four loaders: wheel loader (swallows gravel heaps), backhoe loader (a digger riding on a loader\u2019s back), motor grader (shaves ground flat) and skid-steer (tiny, fast, spins on the spot).',
  mean:'All four exist for one verb — move material — which makes this the set\u2019s cleanest compare-and-contrast: same job, four bodies. The skid-steer\u2019s pirouette is the crowd favourite.',
  know:'You need the card, pom-poms as gravel, a scoop or dustpan, and one leg for the pivot test. The relay does the teaching.',
  why:'Same-verb-different-body comparison builds real analysis: children stop seeing "diggers" and start seeing four tools for one job — each best at a different corner of it.',
  dev:'Comparative analysis, scoop-and-carry motor control, pivot balance (the skid-steer test), and material-handling vocabulary.',
  vocab:'wheel loader, backhoe, motor grader, skid-steer, scoop, gravel, spin',
  teach:'1) The squad: "wheel loader — one BIG bite of gravel! Backhoe — a digger riding a loader! Grader — shaves the ground flat as a pancake. Skid-steer — the little one that SPINS on the spot!"\n2) The relay: pom-pom gravel moved scoop-ful by scoop-ful — loader style, big bites only.\n3) The backhoe double: "two machines in one — digger behind, loader in front. It does BOTH jobs!"\n4) The pivot: stand on one foot, spin slowly — "a skid-steer pirouette! It turns on a coin, in the mud."\n5) The shave: the grader\u2019s job is the FLAT — "smooth ground is the first step of every building."',
  say:'"Wheel loader — CHOMP, a big bite! Backhoe — digger on a loader\u2019s back, two machines in one! Grader — shave, shave, flat as a pancake. Skid-steer — spin, spin on the spot, the dancer of the site! Four bodies, one job: MOVE IT."',
  notice:'On this card the heading "Construction Vehicles" sits over a wheel loader, a backhoe loader, a motor grader and a skid-steer — the scoop squad, lined up.',
  ask:'"Which one spins?" (skid-steer!) "Which is two machines in one?" (backhoe) "What does the grader make?" (flat ground) "Move the gravel — how?" (scoop, big bites!)',
  childSay:'Your child might pivot-spin through the kitchen, scoop-relay every pom-pom in the house, or spot backhoes on every street — the squad, hired.',
  real:'Every housing site runs the squad: loaders biting, graders shaving, skid-steers dancing between them. One fence-line view covers the card.',
  play:'Gravel Company: pom-poms mined from one bowl, delivered to another, flat-shaved at the end (the grader\u2019s finish). Full shift, full pride, full nap.',
  check:'Ask: "The one verb they share?" (move it!) "Who spins?" "Who is two machines?" — squad, verb, spin: compared and known.'},
 'construction-03':{
  title:'The Drilling Rig, Pile Driver, Telehandler & Boom Lift Lesson',one:'Teach deep and high — down into the ground, up into the sky.',
  seoTitle:'Drilling Rig & Boom Lift Lesson for Ages 4–5 — Deep and High (Class 24)',
  metaDescription:'A step-by-step transportation lesson for ages 4–5: teach drilling rig, pile driver, telehandler and boom lift — the machines that drill down, hammer deep and reach up higher than trees.',
  intro:'This lesson teaches direction by machine: two go DOWN (drill, hammer), two go UP (telescope arm, basket lift) — and the BAM of the pile driver is the loudest lesson on the card.',
  what:'Four deep-and-high machines: drilling rig (bores down), pile driver (hammers giant nails into the earth), telehandler (telescopes out AND up) and boom lift (carries people in a basket to the sky).',
  mean:'Foundations are invisible once built — the pile driver\u2019s hammering is one of the few construction acts children get to HEAR. Direction (down/up) gives the card its clean structure.',
  know:'You need the card, a spoon "drill," a soft hammer "pile driver," and a tiptoe "telehandler" reach. The BAM question — why hammer giant nails? — is the depth.',
  why:'Tall buildings need strong grips on the earth: piles are the secret underneath every skyline. Children who know the BAM know what holds the city up.',
  dev:'Direction vocabulary (down, up), sound-to-machine mapping (BAM!), reach measurement (how high?), and the foundations-under-everything idea.',
  vocab:'drilling rig, pile driver, telehandler, boom lift, drill, hammer, reach, basket',
  teach:'1) Two down, two up: "drilling rig — DOWN, down, down into the ground. Pile driver — BAM, giant nails! Telehandler — OUT and UP, a telescope arm. Boom lift — a BASKET to the sky!"\n2) Act them: spoon-drill the earth (a plant pot), soft-hammer BAM a "pile" (a dowel), tiptoe-reach higher and higher, teddy rides the "basket" (your hands).\n3) The BAM question: "why hammer giant nails into the earth? Tall buildings need strong GRIPS — the piles hold everything up."\n4) The reach record: how high is the real telehandler? Six houses high. Measure your house — multiply!\n5) The site sound: next hammering site, name it from afar: "the pile driver! BAM, BAM — building grips."',
  say:'"Drilling rig — down, down, DOWN! Pile driver — BAM, BAM, giant nails into the earth! Telehandler — out and UP, a telescope arm! Boom lift — a basket to the sky, higher than the trees! Deep and high — that is how cities stand."',
  notice:'On this card the heading "Construction Vehicles" sits over a drilling rig, a pile driver mid-BAM, a telehandler with its telescope arm and a boom lift\u2019s raised basket — deep and high, drawn working.',
  ask:'"Which two go DOWN?" (drill, pile driver) "Which go UP?" (telehandler, boom lift) "What does the BAM build?" (grips for tall buildings!)',
  childSay:'Your child might BAM spoons responsibly, measure reaches against houses, or explain piles from the back seat — direction, engineered.',
  real:'City sites drive piles weekly — the BAM carries across whole blocks. Sky platforms (boom lifts) wash every tall window in town.',
  play:'Deep-and-High Course: drill (spoon), BAM (soft hammer), reach (tiptoe record), basket (teddy lifted) — four stations, certified down-and-up operator.',
  check:'Ask: "Sort the four by direction." "What holds tall buildings up?" (the piles — BAM!) "How high does the boom lift go?" — down, up, why: deep and high.'},
 'construction-04':{
  title:'The Scissor Lift, Material Lift, Tunnel Borer & Trencher Lesson',one:'Teach up, across and through — folding lifts, mountain-chewers and line-drawers.',
  seoTitle:'Tunnel Boring Machine Lesson for Ages 4–5 — Up and Through (Class 24)',
  metaDescription:'A step-by-step transportation lesson for ages 4–5: teach scissor lift, material lift, tunnel boring machine and trenching machine — folding lifts, the mountain-chewer and the giant pencil that draws ditches.',
  intro:'This lesson teaches up-and-through: lifts that fold like scissors, the tunnel borer that eats through mountains, and the trencher that draws a straight line in the earth like a giant pencil.',
  what:'Four up-and-through machines: scissor lift (folds upward), material lift (hoists loads up scaffolds), tunnel boring machine (chews tunnels — the set\u2019s biggest vehicle) and trenching machine (cuts long straight ditches).',
  mean:'The tunnel borer is the biggest machine in the entire class — some as long as a football field — and its cousin is the worm in the apple. Same idea, city scale.',
  know:'You need the card, scissor-hands (open-close like the lift), a blanket "mountain" to crawl through, and chalk for the trencher\u2019s straight line.',
  why:'Underground construction is invisible in real life — the blanket-tunnel game makes it edible. The scissor-finger mechanism is also a favourite hand exercise.',
  dev:'Mechanism imitation (scissor folding), through-tunnel crawling, straight-line drawing, and the underground-city idea (pipes and trains below!).',
  vocab:'scissor lift, material lift, tunnel boring machine, trencher, fold, tunnel, ditch',
  teach:'1) Up first: "scissor lift — folds UP like scissors! Material lift — hoists loads up the scaffold."\n2) Scissor-hands: open-close, open-close, rising — "that is the lift\u2019s whole secret — folding scissors!"\n3) Through: "the tunnel borer — CRUNCH, CRUNCH — chews a tunnel through a MOUNTAIN. It is the biggest machine in this whole class!"\n4) The blanket mountain: crawl through while a partner chants "crunch, crunch, keep boring!" — the tunnel game.\n5) The line: "the trencher draws a long straight ditch — a giant pencil! Pipes and cables ride in its line."',
  say:'"Scissor lift — folds UP, like your hands! Material lift — hoist, hoist, up the scaffold! Tunnel borer — CRUNCH through the mountain, the biggest machine in the class! Trencher — draws a straight ditch, a giant pencil. Up, up, THROUGH, and line!"',
  notice:'On this card the heading "Construction Vehicles" sits over a scissor lift rising, a material lift at its scaffold, a tunnel borer\u2019s giant face and a trenching machine — up and through.',
  ask:'"What folds like scissors?" (the lift) "What chews tunnels?" (the borer — biggest machine!) "What draws the ditch?" (the trencher — a giant pencil!)',
  childSay:'Your child might scissor-hand everything upward, blanket-tunnel on demand, or chalk trench-lines down the pavement — up and through, mastered.',
  real:'Metro tunnels are bored under most big cities — the machines make the news when they break through. Scissor lifts park at every shopping-centre ceiling repair.',
  play:'The Boring Company: sofa-cushion mountains, blanket tunnels, a crawling crew and a chant. The tunnel opens; the train (the child) drives through; the company declares dividends.',
  check:'Ask: "What folds up?" (scissor lift) "What chews through mountains?" (the borer!) "What draws straight ditches?" (the trencher) — fold, chew, draw: up and through.'},
 'construction-05':{
  title:'The Concrete Pump, Water Truck, Lowboy & Batching Plant Lesson',one:'Teach the support fleet — the machines that feed the heroes.',
  seoTitle:'Concrete Pump Truck Lesson for Ages 4–5 — The Support Fleet (Class 24)',
  metaDescription:'A step-by-step transportation lesson for ages 4–5: teach concrete pump truck, water truck, lowboy trailer and mobile batching plant — the support crew that feeds every building site, one straw-arm at a time.',
  intro:'This lesson teaches the quiet heroes: the pump truck pouring concrete through a folding arm like a giant straw, the dust-settling water truck, the flat lowboy carrying giants and the batching plant mixing on the spot.',
  what:'Four support machines: concrete pump truck (long folding arm), water truck (sprinkles dust flat), lowboy trailer (carries the giant machines) and mobile batching plant (mixes concrete on site).',
  mean:'None of these build anything — and none of the others could build without them. The support-fleet idea is a first lesson in how work really gets done: the crew behind the crew comes first.',
  know:'You need the card, a bendy straw (the pump trick), a spray bottle (water truck) and the flat-truck question: "why is the lowboy SO low?"',
  why:'The straw demo — moving liquid through a long folding arm — makes the pump truck\u2019s genius drinkable. The lowboy answers the highway question: why is that truck so flat? So the giants can climb on.',
  dev:'Support-role thinking, straw-fluid demonstration, sprinkling control, the low-flat-for-loading reason, and on-site mixing logistics.',
  vocab:'concrete pump, water truck, lowboy, batching plant, arm, sprinkle, flat',
  teach:'1) The fleet: "pump truck — pours concrete through a folding arm, like a giant STRAW! Water truck — sprinkle, sprinkle, the dust stays down. Lowboy — SO flat, it carries the giant machines. Batching plant — mixes concrete RIGHT on the spot."\n2) The straw demo: sip through a long bendy straw — "that is the pump arm: liquid, delivered far away!"\n3) The sprinkle: mist the dusty sandbox — "the water truck\u2019s job: dust down, air clean."\n4) The lowboy question: "why SO low and flat? Ramps! The excavators climb on." (Connect to construction-08\u2019s hauler.)\n5) The support salute: "before the heroes build, the support crew arrives. Power, water, concrete, transport — ALL ready."',
  say:'"Pump truck — slurp, concrete through the giant straw-arm! Water truck — sprinkle, the dust stays down. Lowboy — flat and low, the giants climb aboard! Batching plant — mixing concrete RIGHT THERE. The heroes get the glory — the support fleet makes it possible!"',
  notice:'On this card the heading "Construction Vehicles" sits over a pump truck with its folded arm, a water truck, a flat lowboy trailer and a mobile batching plant — the support fleet, standing by.',
  ask:'"What does the pump arm do?" (pours concrete far away) "Why is the lowboy so low?" (ramps for the giants!) "What does the water truck settle?" (the dust!)',
  childSay:'Your child might straw-demo every drink, spray-bottle "water-truck" the garden, or load toys onto flat cushions — support thinking, running.',
  real:'Every big site hosts the fleet: pump arms reaching second floors, water trucks circling, lowboys delivering diggers at dawn. Dawn deliveries are the best spectating.',
  play:'Support Run: the toys build a "tower" — the straw-arm pours (water into a cup), the spray-bottle settles the dust, the flat cushion delivers the builder. The tower rises on support.',
  check:'Ask: "Who pours concrete far away?" (the pump truck) "Who carries the giants?" (the lowboy) "Who comes first — heroes or support?" (support!) — arm, sprinkle, flat: the fleet behind the crew.'},
 'construction-06':{
  title:'The Street Sweeper, Snowplow, Line Painter & Rail Grinder Lesson',one:'Teach the keepers — the tidy-up crew of roads and rails.',
  seoTitle:'Street Sweeper & Snowplow Lesson for Ages 4–5 — The Keepers (Class 24)',
  metaDescription:'A step-by-step transportation lesson for ages 4–5: teach street sweeper, snowplow, pavement marking machine and rail grinder — the tidy-up crew, from brushed gutters to rail-grinding sparks.',
  intro:'This lesson teaches the maintainers: the sweeper that brushes, the plow that shoves white waves, the painter that draws the road\u2019s lines and the grinder that showers sparks to keep tracks smooth.',
  what:'Four keeping machines: street sweeper (brushes gutters), snowplow (moves snow), pavement marking machine (paints lines) and rail grinder (sparks — shaves the tracks smooth).',
  mean:'These machines exist to keep everything ELSE working — a quietly beautiful idea to hand a child: roads need brushing, snow needs pushing, lines need painting. Maintenance is a form of care.',
  know:'You need the card, masking-tape roads and chalk (the line-painting job), and the sparks question: "why does the rail grinder make FIREWORKS?"',
  why:'The rail grinder\u2019s sparks are the most impressive tidying-up on Earth — and the line-painting job is a genuine fine-motor activity the machine does at walking pace.',
  dev:'Maintenance-as-care thinking, straight-line painting (fine motor), weather-response vocabulary (snow!), and the spark explanation (metal on metal).',
  vocab:'street sweeper, snowplow, line painter, rail grinder, brush, plow, paint, sparks',
  teach:'1) The keepers: "sweeper — brush, brush, clean gutters. Snowplow — WHOOSH, mountains of snow move aside! Line painter — the road\u2019s long yellow lines, drawn at walking pace. Rail grinder — SPARKS! The tracks get a shave."\n2) The painting job: tape a road, chalk the centre line — "straight lines are a real skill; the machine does it slowly, so can you."\n3) The sparks question: "grinding metal makes sparks — the track\u2019s bumps get ground smooth, or trains bump and bang."\n4) The weather crew: "when it snows, the plows work ALL NIGHT so the morning can happen."\n5) The thanks: "these machines exist so everything else works. The quietest heroes on the road."',
  say:'"Street sweeper — brush brush! Snowplow — WHOOSH, the snow moves! Line painter — the long yellow line, drawn slow and straight. Rail grinder — SPARKS, the tracks get a shave! The keepers: roads brushed, snow pushed, lines painted, rails smooth."',
  notice:'On this card the heading "Construction Vehicles" sits over a street sweeper, a snowplow, a pavement marking machine and a rail grinder mid-sparks — the keepers, on shift.',
  ask:'"Who brushes the streets?" "Who pushes the snow?" "What makes the sparks?" (the rail grinder!) "What does the line painter draw?"',
  childSay:'Your child might chalk perfect road lines, plow snow-piles with toy trucks, or gasp at real rail-grinding sparks — maintenance, admired.',
  real:'Sweepers circle at dawn, plows run all night in snow country, line painters close lanes on Sundays, and rail grinders spark at midnight. The keepers keep odd hours.',
  play:'Keeper Shift: sweep the rug (crumbs!), plow the "snow" (paper scraps), tape-and-chalk a road with proper lines, finish with a spark dance. The whole shift, one living room.',
  check:'Ask: "Name the keepers." "Why the sparks?" (grinding metal smooth!) "What would happen with no snowplows?" — brush, plow, paint, sparks: care with wheels.'},
 'construction-07':{
  title:'The Cold Planer, Asphalt Recycler, Curb Machine & Slipform Paver Lesson',one:'Teach the road doctors — shave, recycle, squeeze and pour: roads born again.',
  seoTitle:'Cold Planer & Slipform Paver Lesson for Ages 4–5 — The Road Doctors (Class 24)',
  metaDescription:'A step-by-step transportation lesson for ages 4–5: teach cold planer, asphalt recycler, curb machine and slipform paver — the machines that fix roads, and the recycling lesson hiding in them.',
  intro:'This lesson teaches the road doctors: the planer that shaves old bumpy surface like a giant cheese grater, the recycler that melts it into NEW road, the curb machine that squeezes out sidewalk edges and the paver that glides fresh lanes into being.',
  what:'Four road-fixing machines: cold planer (shaves old surface), asphalt recycler (melts chewings into new road), curb machine (extrudes the pavement edge) and slipform paver (pours fresh smooth lanes in one glide).',
  mean:'This card is the construction set\u2019s recycling lesson: old road is not rubbish — it melts and comes back as new road. And the curb machine answers how sidewalks get their raised edge.',
  know:'You need the card, an old crayon and a grater (grown-up hands — the planer demo), and the melt-and-return story for recycling.',
  why:'"Roads can be born again" is recycling at infrastructure scale — concrete, visible, delicious to explain. The crayon-shaving demo makes the planer\u2019s job sensory.',
  dev:'Recycling thinking at big scale, shave-melt-relay sequencing, the curb question (how DID the sidewalk get its edge?), and glide-pour continuity.',
  vocab:'cold planer, recycler, curb machine, slipform paver, shave, melt, curb, lane',
  teach:'1) The doctors: "cold planer — shaves the old bumpy road, like a giant cheese grater! Recycler — MELTS the shavings into new road! Curb machine — squeezes out the sidewalk\u2019s edge. Slipform paver — glides along, leaving fresh smooth lane!"\n2) The crayon demo: grate an old crayon (grown-up) — "shavings! That is the planer\u2019s job — old surface off."\n3) The recycling: "the shavings are not rubbish — they MELT and come back as brand-new road. Roads are born again!"\n4) The curb question: "how did the sidewalk get its raised edge? A machine SQUEEZES it out, like icing!"\n5) The glide: hand-gliding a block across the table leaving a "lane" — the paver\u2019s one-pass magic.',
  say:'"Cold planer — scrape, the old bumpy road comes OFF! Recycler — melt the shavings into NEW road — roads are born again! Curb machine — squeezes the sidewalk\u2019s edge like icing! Slipform paver — glide, glide, fresh smooth lane behind it. The road doctors!"',
  notice:'On this card the heading "Construction Vehicles" sits over a cold planer, an asphalt recycler, a curb machine extruding an edge and a slipform paver laying lane — the road doctors, mid-surgery.',
  ask:'"What shaves the old road?" (the planer) "What happens to the shavings?" (melted — NEW road!) "How does the sidewalk get its edge?" (squeezed out!)',
  childSay:'Your child might grate crayons for science (supervised), explain road-born-again to everyone, or hunt curbs for squeezing evidence — recycling, infrastructure-scale.',
  real:'Road resurfacing closes lanes all summer: planers first, recyclers and pavers behind. Watch one resurfacing job and the whole card drives past.',
  play:'Road Hospital: the toy road is "bumpy" (build obstacles) — shave (grate side of a box, gently), recycle (melted crayon art, grown-up iron on paper), re-pave (smooth paper laid). Discharged, good as new.',
  check:'Ask: "What does the planer do?" (shaves) "What happens to old road?" (born again — recycled!) "Who writes curbs?" (the curb machine) — shave, melt, squeeze, glide: roads mended.'},
 'construction-08':{
  title:'The Articulated Hauler, Motor Scraper, Pipe Layer & Trench Compactor Lesson',one:'Teach the earthmovers — bendy haulers, ground-shavers, pipe-layers and stampers.',
  seoTitle:'Articulated Hauler & Pipe Layer Lesson for Ages 4–5 — The Earthmovers (Class 24)',
  metaDescription:'A step-by-step transportation lesson for ages 4–5: teach articulated hauler, motor scraper, pipe layer and trench compactor — the heavy crew that moves earth, lays the water\u2019s secret roads and stamps the ground hard.',
  intro:'This lesson teaches the earthmovers: the hauler that bends to carry mountains, the scraper that eats ground into its own belly, the pipe layer threading the water\u2019s secret roads and the compactor stamping the earth firm.',
  what:'Four earthmoving machines: articulated hauler (bends, hauls tons), motor scraper (shaves ground into itself), pipe layer (lowers pipes into trenches) and trench compactor (stamps earth hard).',
  mean:'The bend-in-the-middle repeat — the same hinge as the articulated bus, doing far harder work. And the pipe layer reveals infrastructure\u2019s secret: beneath every road runs a hidden water city.',
  know:'You need the card, two toy trucks and tape (the bendy-hauler build), and the big question: "where does the tap water come from?"',
  why:'The invisible city beneath the road — pipes, sewers, cables — is a genuine revelation to children. The pipe layer makes it visible; the compactor explains why the ground must be stamped hard first.',
  dev:'Articulation reuse (bus → hauler), hidden-infrastructure thinking, stamping motor work (compaction!), and shave-into-belly mechanics.',
  vocab:'hauler, motor scraper, pipe layer, compactor, bend, pipes, stamp',
  teach:'1) The crew: "hauler — bends in the MIDDLE, carries a mountain of dirt! Motor scraper — shaves the ground INTO its own belly. Pipe layer — lowers pipes like thread through a needle. Compactor — STAMP, stamp — the earth, hard as a drum."\n2) The bend build: tape two trucks with a paper hinge — "it bends! The same trick as the articulated bus — but hauling MUD."\n3) The secret roads: "under every street run water pipes and sewers — the pipe layer lays them. The water\u2019s secret roads!"\n4) The stamp test: stamp the sandbox with both feet — firm! "Loose ground squishes; stamped ground HOLDS roads up."\n5) The scraper\u2019s trick: "it shaves the ground and KEEPS it — a belly full of earth, delivered downhill."',
  say:'"Hauler — bends in the middle, hauling a MOUNTAIN! Scraper — shaves the ground into its belly! Pipe layer — the water\u2019s secret roads, laid under every street! Compactor — STAMP, stamp, the earth drum-tight. The earthmovers move the world so the world can be built on it."',
  notice:'On this card the heading "Construction Vehicles" sits over an articulated hauler mid-bend, a motor scraper, a pipe layer lowering pipe and a trench compactor — the earthmovers, ground truth.',
  ask:'"What bends on the hauler?" (its middle!) "Where do the pipes go?" (under the roads — secret water roads!) "Why stamp the earth?" (loose ground squishes — firm holds!)',
  childSay:'Your child might hinge-tape every toy pair, hunt drain covers as "pipe evidence," or stamp-test every sandbox — earthmoving, grounded.',
  real:'Every new suburb is earthmovers first: haulers bending, pipes threading, compactors drumming. The secret city is laid before the houses.',
  play:'Pipe Workers: dig a sandbox trench, lay a "pipe" (pool noodle), cover, stamp firm — then "find" it again with a spoon excavator. The hidden city, met twice.',
  check:'Ask: "Who bends to haul?" (the hauler) "What hides under streets?" (pipes!) "Why stamp?" (firm ground holds) — bend, shave, lay, stamp: the earth obeys.'},
 'construction-09':{
  title:'The Vacuum Excavator, Crawler Carrier, Tracked Carrier & Material Handler Lesson',one:'Teach the clever movers — sucking soil, walking treads and the claw machine.',
  seoTitle:'Vacuum Excavator & Material Handler Lesson for Ages 4–5 — Clever Movers (Class 24)',
  metaDescription:'A step-by-step transportation lesson for ages 4–5: teach vacuum excavator, crawler carrier, tracked carrier and material handler — soil-sucking, tank-tread walking and the biggest claw machine on Earth.',
  intro:'This lesson teaches the clever movers: the vacuum that digs without touching, the tank-tread carriers that walk where wheels sink, and the handler whose grab-arm is the world\u2019s biggest claw machine.',
  what:'Four clever machines: vacuum excavator (sucks soil — digs without touching), crawler and tracked carriers (tank treads for soft ground) and material handler (grab-arm pick and place).',
  mean:'The vacuum excavator is the set\u2019s most surprising machine — digging without touching, so buried pipes stay safe. And the treads answer a nature question: wheels sink in mud; snowshoes and tank treads walk.',
  know:'You need the card, a clothes-peg "grabber" and pom-poms (the claw practice), and the tread question: "why TANK FEET instead of wheels?"',
  why:'Claw-machine pretend is irresistible and it trains exactly the grip the handler uses. The vacuum\u2019s safe-digging idea — look before you dig — is real safety culture, made vivid.',
  dev:'Vacuum/suction concept, tread-vs-wheel physics, peg-grabber fine motor, and the safe-excavation message (call before you dig!).',
  vocab:'vacuum excavator, crawler, tracked carrier, material handler, suction, treads, grab',
  teach:'1) The clever four: "vacuum excavator — SCHLOOP, sucks the soil — digging without touching! Crawler and tracked carriers — TANK FEET, they walk where wheels sink! Material handler — grab, lift, place — the biggest claw machine alive."\n2) The claw practice: clothes-peg pom-pom moves — grab, carry, place on a tower. Handler\u2019s verbs, trained.\n3) The tread question: "why tank feet? In deep mud, WHEELS sink — treads WALK. Nature thought of it first: snowshoes!"\n4) The vacuum\u2019s wisdom: "digging blind can break buried pipes. The vacuum sucks the soil AWAY — the pipes stay safe. Look before you dig!"\n5) The arcade connection: "every claw-machine player knows this machine\u2019s exact joy — and its heartbreak when the grab drops."',
  say:'"Vacuum excavator — SCHLOOP! It sucks the soil — digging without touching, keeping the pipes safe! Crawlers — tank feet, step step over the mud where wheels would sink. Material handler — grab, lift, PLACE — the biggest claw machine in the world!"',
  notice:'On this card the heading "Construction Vehicles" sits over a vacuum excavator with its hose, a crawler carrier, a tracked carrier and a material handler mid-grab — clever movers, drawn thinking.',
  ask:'"Who digs WITHOUT touching?" (the vacuum!) "Why tank feet?" (wheels sink — treads walk!) "What does the handler do?" (grab, lift, place!)',
  childSay:'Your child might peg-grab everything, vacuum-imitate the dustpan, or explain snowshoes via excavators — cleverness, circulating.',
  real:'Utility crews vacuum-dig before every street repair (call before you dig!); crawlers haunt bogs and mines; handlers load scrapyards like enormous arcades.',
  play:'Claw Arcade: peg-grab pom-poms onto a tower, timed rounds, heartbreak drops included — then the vacuum round: a straw "sucks" paper scraps from a tray. Both movers, one arcade.',
  check:'Ask: "Who digs without touching?" (vacuum) "Who walks on tank feet?" (the carriers) "Who plays claw machine for a living?" (the handler!) — suck, walk, grab: cleverness hired.'},
 'construction-10':{
  title:'The Tower Crane, Crawler Crane, Rough-Terrain Crane & Truck Crane Lesson',one:'Teach the crane family — four answers to lifting the heaviest things into the sky.',
  seoTitle:'Tower Crane & Crawler Crane Lesson for Ages 4–5 — The Crane Family (Class 24)',
  metaDescription:'A step-by-step transportation lesson for ages 4–5: teach tower crane, crawler crane, rough-terrain crane and truck-mounted crane — four cranes, four lifting jobs, one balance experiment.',
  intro:'This lesson teaches the crane family: the skyline giant, the treads-and-tonnage lift, the mud-hopping rough-terrain and the drive-itself truck crane — with the counterweight experiment at the centre.',
  what:'Four cranes: tower crane (the skyline signature), crawler crane (heaviest lifts on treads), rough-terrain crane (jumps mud) and truck-mounted crane (drives to every small job).',
  mean:'Four cranes, four answers to one question — how do we lift the heaviest things into the sky? The counterweight is the physics heart: a heavy something on the other side keeps the arm honest.',
  know:'You need the card and the balance game: hold a book at arm\u2019s length (heavy!), then build a spoon crane with a cup counterweight — lift a pom-pom load.',
  why:'Tower cranes are the skyline\u2019s signature and children spot them from farther than any other machine. The counterweight experiment turns wonder into mechanics.',
  dev:'Lifting-mechanics thinking (counterweight!), height comparison, terrain-to-crane matching, and arm-hold endurance (the honest way to feel a crane).',
  vocab:'tower crane, crawler crane, rough-terrain crane, truck crane, lift, counterweight, load',
  teach:'1) The family: "tower crane — the TALLEST, it watches the whole city! Crawler crane — treads, and the HEAVIEST lifts. Rough-terrain crane — hops the mud. Truck crane — drives itself to every little job."\n2) The height sort: which is tallest? (Tower — often higher than the building it builds!)\n3) The arm test: hold a book at arm\u2019s length — "heavy, right? Cranes do that ALL DAY — with a secret: the COUNTERWEIGHT."\n4) The spoon crane: spoon arm, tape pivot, cup counterweight — "heavy cup one side… pom-pom load lifts easy the other!"\n5) The spot: tower cranes rule every skyline — "count them from the window. Every one is building something."',
  say:'"Tower crane — tallest of all, king of the skyline! Crawler crane — treads and TONNAGE, the heaviest lifts! Rough-terrain crane — hops the mud! Truck crane — drives itself to little jobs. And the secret of every crane: the COUNTERWEIGHT — heavy one side, load flies the other!"',
  notice:'On this card the heading "Construction Vehicles" sits over a tall tower crane, a tracked crawler crane, a rough-terrain crane and a truck-mounted crane — the family, rigged and ready.',
  ask:'"Which crane is tallest?" (tower!) "Which lifts heaviest?" (crawler) "What balances the load?" (the counterweight!)',
  childSay:'Your child might count tower cranes from every window, spoon-crane everything, or explain counterweights at the playground — the family, spotted and understood.',
  real:'Skylines are crane calendars: count them and count the construction. Crawler cranes assemble wind turbines; truck cranes fix every rooftop.',
  play:'Crane School: build the spoon crane, lift loads of increasing weight, adjust the counterweight until it balances — then certify the operator. The skyline awaits.',
  check:'Ask: "Match each crane to its job." "What balances the arm?" (counterweight!) "Which is tallest?" (tower) — family, physics, skyline: lifting, mastered.'},
 'construction-11':{
  title:'The Generator, Light Tower, Compressor & Welding Truck Lesson',one:'Teach the power crew — hum, glow, pshhh and bzzz: the site\u2019s soundtrack and its source.',
  seoTitle:'Generator & Welding Truck Lesson for Ages 4–5 — The Power Crew (Class 24)',
  metaDescription:'A step-by-step transportation lesson for ages 4–5: teach generator, light tower, compressor and welding trucks — the trailers that bring power, light, air and sparks, and the support-crew lesson that ends the class.',
  intro:'This lesson closes the construction shelf with its quiet heroes: four trailers that bring the electricity, the light, the air and the sparks — and the big idea that support comes first.',
  what:'Four power machines: generator trailer (site electricity), light tower trailer (lamps that turn night into day), compressor trailer (air for the tools) and welding truck (joins steel with sparks).',
  mean:'None of them build anything — and none of the builders could build without them. Power, light, air and sparks arrive on four trailers: the support-crew lesson that ends the class where construction-05 began it, deeper.',
  know:'You need the card, a torch on a chair (the light tower) and the sound hunt: the generator HUMS, the compressor PSHHH-es, the welder BZZZ-es.',
  why:'The site has a soundtrack — hum, pshhh, bzzz — and each sound is a machine feeding the others. Children who can hear the support crew can hear how work really works.',
  dev:'Sound-to-machine mapping, power-source thinking (where does site electricity COME FROM?), night-shift wonder (light towers), and the support-first idea, completed.',
  vocab:'generator, light tower, compressor, welding truck, power, sparks, hum',
  teach:'1) The crew: "generator — HUMMM, it makes the electricity! Light tower — lifts its lamps, night becomes day! Compressor — PSHHH, squeezed air for the tools. Welding truck — BZZZ, joins steel with sparks!"\n2) The sound hunt: make the three sounds — "which machine says each?"\n3) The night question: "how do builders work at night? The light tower — lamps up, site glowing."\n4) The power source: "no plugs on a building site — the GENERATOR makes the electricity. It hums so everything else can work."\n5) The closing idea: "remember the support fleet? This is its heart: before the builders build, someone brings the power. The support crew comes FIRST."',
  say:'"Generator — hummm, the site\u2019s electricity! Light tower — lamps UP, night turns to day! Compressor — pshhh, air for the tools! Welding truck — bzzz, sparks join the steel! None of them build — and none of the builders could build without them. Support first!"',
  notice:'On this card the heading "Construction Vehicles" sits over a generator trailer, a light tower raised, a compressor trailer and a welding truck — the power crew, humming.',
  ask:'"Which machine hums?" (generator) "Which turns night to day?" (light tower) "Which makes sparks?" (the welder) "Who comes first?" (support!)',
  childSay:'Your child might hum-generate through the house, torch-tower every dark corner, or bzzz-weld toy steel — the soundtrack, running.',
  real:'Every site hums, glows, hisses and sparks after dark — drive past one at night and the whole card is audible. Generators also power festivals and film sets.',
  play:'Night Shift: torch light-tower, hum the generator, pshhh the compressor, bzzz-weld the toy frames (spark fingers) — build a block tower BY LAMPLIGHT. The site, powered by its crew.',
  check:'Ask: "Name the power crew." "What does the generator make?" (electricity!) "What did we learn about support?" (it comes first!) — hum, glow, pshhh, bzzz: the class closes on its quietest heroes.'}
};